addSubject({id:'phy', topics:[{"t": "Topic 1 – Properties of light: propagation, reflection, refraction", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain shadows by rectilinear propagation</li><li>draw ray diagrams for plane and curved mirrors and for lenses</li><li>use 1/u + 1/v = 1/f and M = v/u = hᵢ/hₒ</li><li>use n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ and find the critical angle</li><li>compare concave/convex mirrors and converging/diverging lenses</li></ul></div><h3>1. How we see things</h3>\n<p>Light travels in <b>straight lines</b> called <b>rays</b>. We see an object when light from it (or reflected off it) enters our eyes. Light travels at 3 × 10⁸ m/s in air.</p>\n<h3>2. Laws of reflection</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 170\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><g stroke=\"#9aa6c0\" stroke-width=\"1\"><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"15\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"35\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"55\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"165\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"185\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"205\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"215\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"245\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"265\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"152\"/><line x1=\"285\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"275\" y2=\"152\"/></g><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,4\"/><text x=\"164\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#555\">normal</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><polygon points=\"110,90 102,78 116,84\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><polygon points=\"215,85 222,72 209,78\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><path d=\"M160 110 A30 30 0 0 0 139 119\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"130\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c0392b\">i</text><path d=\"M160 110 A30 30 0 0 1 181 119\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\"/><text x=\"183\" y=\"105\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">r</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"36\" font-size=\"11\">incident ray</text><text x=\"225\" y=\"36\" font-size=\"11\">reflected ray</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"162\" font-size=\"11\">plane mirror</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Angle of incidence i = angle of reflection r (both measured from the NORMAL)</div></div>\n<ol><li>The angle of incidence = the angle of reflection (<b>i = r</b>).</li><li>The incident ray, the reflected ray and the normal all lie in the <b>same plane</b>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"warn\">⚠ Always measure angles from the <b>normal</b> (the dashed line at 90° to the mirror), NOT from the mirror. If a ray hits the mirror at 30° to the surface, i = 60°.</div>\n<h3>3. Image in a plane mirror</h3>\n<p>The image is: <b>virtual</b> (cannot be caught on a screen), <b>upright</b>, the <b>same size</b> as the object, <b>laterally inverted</b> (left and right swapped), and as far <b>behind</b> the mirror as the object is in front.</p>\n<h3>4. Curved mirrors</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 170\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#888\"/><path d=\"M290 15 Q250 85 290 155\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"85\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"195\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c0392b\">F</text><circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"85\" r=\"3\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"12\">C</text><g stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"268\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"268\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"140\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"268\" y2=\"125\"/><line x1=\"268\" y1=\"125\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"30\"/></g><text x=\"12\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"11\">Concave mirror: parallel rays reflect THROUGH the focus F</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"10\">f = focal length (pole → F)</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A concave (converging) mirror; C = centre of curvature = 2f</div></div>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Concave (converging)</th><th>Convex (diverging)</th></tr><tr><td>Shape</td><td>Caves inward</td><td>Bulges outward</td></tr><tr><td>Image</td><td>Real or virtual (depends on the object's position)</td><td>Always virtual, upright, smaller</td></tr><tr><td>Uses</td><td>Torch reflector, shaving/make-up mirror, satellite dish</td><td>Car side mirror, shop security mirror (wide view)</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Ray rules for curved mirrors</h4>\n<ul><li>A ray parallel to the axis reflects <b>through F</b> (concave) or as if from F (convex).</li><li>A ray through F reflects <b>parallel</b> to the axis.</li><li>A ray through C reflects straight back.</li></ul>\n<h3>5. Mirror formula and magnification</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">1/f = 1/u + 1/v<br>m = hᵢ/hₒ = v/u<br>f = R/2</div>\n<p>u = object distance, v = image distance, f = focal length. A <b>positive v</b> means a <b>real</b> image (in front of the mirror). A negative v means virtual. For a convex mirror, f is negative.</p>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A concave mirror has f = 10 cm. An object is 30 cm away. 1/v = 1/10 − 1/30 = 2/30 → v = 15 cm (real). m = 15/30 = 0.5, so the image is half the size and inverted.</div>\n<div class=\"tip\">💡 KSSC often asks you to \"show that\" a value is correct. Always write the formula, substitute with units, and give the final answer to the same number of significant figures.</div><h3>6. Refraction</h3>\n<p>Refraction is the <b>bending of light</b> when it passes from one medium to another, because its <b>speed changes</b>. Going into a <b>denser</b> medium (air → glass) light slows down and bends <b>towards</b> the normal. Going into a less dense medium it speeds up and bends <b>away from</b> the normal.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 200\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"100\" width=\"300\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#dbeafe\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"12\">air (n = 1.00)</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"190\" font-size=\"12\">glass (n = 1.5)</text><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,4\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"185\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M150 70 A30 30 0 0 0 129 79\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"125\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c0392b\">θ₁</text><path d=\"M150 135 A35 35 0 0 0 165 132\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"152\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">θ₂</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"150\" font-size=\"11\">bends TOWARDS normal</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Light slows down in glass, so θ₂ &lt; θ₁</div></div>\n<div class=\"key\">Snell's law: n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂<br>n = c / v = sin i / sin r</div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Light enters water (n = 1.33) from air at 40°. sin θ₂ = sin 40°/1.33 = 0.483 → θ₂ = 28.9°.</div>\n<h3>7. Total internal reflection (TIR)</h3>\n<p>When light goes from a <b>denser to a less dense</b> medium (glass → air), the refracted ray bends away from the normal. As the angle increases, the refracted ray runs <b>along the surface</b> (90°). That angle of incidence is the <b>critical angle c</b>. Above c, <b>all</b> the light reflects back: this is total internal reflection.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 150\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"360\" height=\"75\" fill=\"#dbeafe\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"11\">glass</text><text x=\"5\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"11\">air</text><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">i < c</text><text x=\"35\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"10\">refracts out</text><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"235\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"160\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">i = c</text><text x=\"155\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"10\">along surface</text><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"140\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><line x1=\"245\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"300\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"355\" y2=\"5\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">i > c</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"124\" font-size=\"10\">TIR</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The three cases at a glass–air boundary</div></div>\n<div class=\"key\">sin c = 1/n  (light going from the medium into air)</div>\n<p>Conditions for TIR: (1) light travels from the denser to the less dense medium; (2) the angle of incidence is greater than the critical angle.</p>\n<p>Uses: <b>optical fibres</b> (internet cables, endoscopes for seeing inside the body), prism binoculars, and the sparkle of diamonds (c = 24°).</p>\n<h3>8. Convex and concave lenses</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 170\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"85\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"85\" stroke=\"#888\"/><ellipse cx=\"170\" cy=\"85\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"70\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"85\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c0392b\">F</text><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"85\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"85\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c0392b\">F</text><g stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2\"><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"40\"/><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"125\"/><line x1=\"15\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"130\"/><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"45\"/></g><text x=\"15\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"11\">Convex (converging) lens: parallel rays meet at F</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A convex lens brings light to a focus. A concave lens spreads it out.</div></div>\n<h4>Three rays for drawing lens diagrams</h4>\n<ol><li>Parallel to the axis → refracts through F on the other side.</li><li>Through the centre of the lens → goes straight on (no bending).</li><li>Through F on the near side → comes out parallel.</li></ol>\n<table><tr><th>Object position (convex lens)</th><th>Image</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>Beyond 2F</td><td>Real, inverted, smaller, between F and 2F</td><td>Camera, eye</td></tr><tr><td>At 2F</td><td>Real, inverted, same size, at 2F</td><td>Photocopier</td></tr><tr><td>Between F and 2F</td><td>Real, inverted, larger, beyond 2F</td><td>Projector</td></tr><tr><td>Inside F</td><td>Virtual, upright, larger, same side</td><td>Magnifying glass</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"key\">Lens formula: 1/f = 1/u + 1/v<br>Power P = 1/f (f in metres, P in dioptres)</div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Shadows form because light…", "o": ["bends around objects", "travels in straight lines", "is a sound wave", "reflects from shadows"], "a": 1, "e": "Rectilinear propagation: opaque objects block the straight rays."}, {"q": "The image in a plane mirror is…", "o": ["real and inverted", "virtual and laterally inverted", "real and magnified", "virtual and smaller"], "a": 1, "e": "Virtual, upright, same size, laterally inverted."}, {"q": "Angle of incidence = 35°. The angle of reflection is…", "o": ["55°", "35°", "70°", "145°"], "a": 1, "e": "i = r."}, {"q": "Which image can be shown on a screen?", "o": ["Virtual", "Real", "Both", "Neither"], "a": 1, "e": "Real rays actually converge there."}, {"q": "Partial reflection means…", "o": ["all light is absorbed", "some light is reflected and some is transmitted", "all light is reflected", "light bends"], "a": 1, "e": "E.g. light hitting glass."}, {"q": "Angles of incidence and reflection are measured from…", "o": ["the mirror surface", "the normal", "the horizontal", "the image"], "a": 1, "e": "The normal is perpendicular to the surface."}, {"q": "Light goes from air (1.00) into water (1.33) at 20°. Angle of refraction?", "o": ["14.9°", "20.0°", "27.1°", "40.0°"], "a": 0, "e": "sin r = sin 20°/1.33 = 0.257 → 14.9°."}, {"q": "Total internal reflection needs light to go…", "o": ["from less dense to denser", "from denser to less dense, above the critical angle", "at 0°", "through a vacuum"], "a": 1, "e": "Both conditions must be met."}, {"q": "A concave lens forms images that are…", "o": ["real and inverted", "virtual, upright and smaller", "real and larger", "always at F"], "a": 1, "e": "Diverging lenses only form virtual images."}, {"q": "Critical angle in glass with n = 1.5", "o": ["41.8°", "48.6°", "30°", "60°"], "a": 0, "e": "sin C = 1/1.5 → 41.8°."}], "w": 12}, {"t": "Topic 2 – Wave properties and propagation", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe pulses and periodic waves, transverse and longitudinal</li><li>use v = fλ and f = 1/T</li><li>describe sound as a longitudinal wave and waves as energy carriers</li><li>describe wavefronts and rays in a ripple tank</li><li>state that light is an electromagnetic wave</li></ul></div><h3>1. What is a wave?</h3>\n<p>A wave carries <b>energy</b> from one place to another <b>without carrying matter</b>. The particles only vibrate about their rest position.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 150\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"#999\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/><path d=\"M10 75 L10 75 L14 68.74262139839077 L18 62.6393202250021 L22 56.84038001041813 L26 51.48858990830107 L30 46.7157287525381 L34 42.6393202250021 L38 39.359739032465285 L42 36.95773934819386 L46 35.492466376194486 L50 35 L54 35.49246637619449 L58 36.957739348193854 L62 39.359739032465285 L66 42.6393202250021 L70 46.7157287525381 L74 51.488589908301066 L78 56.84038001041813 L82 62.6393202250021 L86 68.74262139839077 L90 75 L94 81.25737860160923 L98 87.3606797749979 L102 93.15961998958184 L106 98.51141009169892 L110 103.2842712474619 L114 107.36067977499789 L118 110.64026096753472 L122 113.04226065180615 L126 114.5075336238055 L130 115 L134 114.50753362380551 L138 113.04226065180615 L142 110.64026096753472 L146 107.3606797749979 L150 103.2842712474619 L154 98.51141009169893 L158 93.15961998958187 L162 87.3606797749979 L166 81.25737860160925 L170 75.00000000000001 L174 68.74262139839081 L178 62.63932022500211 L182 56.84038001041814 L186 51.48858990830106 L190 46.715728752538105 L194 42.63932022500213 L198 39.359739032465285 L202 36.95773934819386 L206 35.492466376194486 L210 35 L214 35.492466376194486 L218 36.957739348193854 L222 39.359739032465264 L226 42.639320225002095 L230 46.71572875253807 L234 51.488589908301066 L238 56.840380010418144 L242 62.63932022500209 L246 68.74262139839078 L250 74.99999999999999 L254 81.25737860160919 L258 87.36067977499788 L262 93.15961998958183 L266 98.5114100916989 L270 103.28427124746192 L274 107.36067977499789 L278 110.64026096753472 L282 113.04226065180615 L286 114.50753362380551 L290 115 L294 114.50753362380551 L298 113.04226065180615 L302 110.64026096753474 L306 107.3606797749979 L310 103.28427124746193 L314 98.51141009169893 L318 93.15961998958186 L322 87.36067977499792 L326 81.25737860160922 L330 75.00000000000001\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"25\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c0392b\">wavelength λ</text><line x1=\"290\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"295\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">amplitude A</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"11\">crest</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"132\" font-size=\"11\">trough</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A transverse wave</div></div>\n<table><tr><th>Transverse</th><th>Longitudinal</th></tr><tr><td>Particles vibrate at <b>90°</b> to the direction the wave travels</td><td>Particles vibrate <b>parallel</b> to the direction the wave travels</td></tr><tr><td>Crests and troughs</td><td>Compressions and rarefactions</td></tr><tr><td>Light, water waves, waves on a rope</td><td>Sound, a slinky pushed back and forth</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 70\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"10\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"20.72394299843728\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"20.72394299843728\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"30.854101966249686\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"30.854101966249686\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"39.92613004357082\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"39.92613004357082\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"47.70633909777092\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"47.70633909777092\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"54.242640687119284\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"54.242640687119284\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"59.85410196624969\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"59.85410196624969\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"65.06139320975862\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"65.06139320975862\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"70.47328848624517\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"70.47328848624517\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"76.6539608548698\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"76.6539608548698\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"84\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"84\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"92.6539608548698\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"92.6539608548698\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"102.47328848624515\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"102.47328848624515\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"113.06139320975862\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"113.06139320975862\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"123.85410196624967\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"123.85410196624967\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"134.2426406871193\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"134.2426406871193\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"143.70633909777092\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"143.70633909777092\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"151.92613004357082\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"151.92613004357082\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"158.8541019662497\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"158.8541019662497\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"164.72394299843728\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"164.72394299843728\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"175.27605700156272\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"175.27605700156272\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"181.1458980337503\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"181.1458980337503\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"188.07386995642918\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"188.07386995642918\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"196.29366090222908\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"196.29366090222908\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"205.7573593128807\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"205.7573593128807\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"216.1458980337503\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"216.1458980337503\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"226.9386067902414\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"226.9386067902414\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"237.52671151375483\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"237.52671151375483\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"247.3460391451302\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"247.3460391451302\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"256\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"256\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"263.3460391451302\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"263.3460391451302\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"269.52671151375483\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"269.52671151375483\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"274.9386067902414\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"274.9386067902414\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"280.1458980337503\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"280.1458980337503\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"285.75735931288074\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"285.75735931288074\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"292.2936609022291\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"292.2936609022291\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"300.0738699564292\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"300.0738699564292\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"309.1458980337503\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"309.1458980337503\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"319.2760570015627\" y1=\"10\" x2=\"319.2760570015627\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"11\">compression (C) – close together; rarefaction (R) – spread apart</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A longitudinal wave (sound)</div></div>\n<h3>2. Wave terms and the wave equation</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Wavelength λ</b> (m): the distance from crest to crest.</li><li><b>Amplitude A</b>: the maximum displacement from rest. Bigger amplitude = more energy (louder sound, brighter light).</li><li><b>Frequency f</b> (Hz): the number of waves per second. <b>Period T</b> = time for one wave = 1/f.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"key\">v = f λ<br>f = 1/T</div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>12 waves pass in 15 s and λ = 0.5 m. f = 12/15 = 0.8 Hz, so v = 0.8 × 0.5 = 0.4 m/s.</div>\n<h3>3. Waves in a ripple tank</h3><p>In a ripple tank, the crests are seen as bright lines called <b>wavefronts</b>. A <b>ray</b> is an arrow drawn at 90° to the wavefronts, showing the direction the wave travels. Straight wavefronts come from a straight bar; circular wavefronts come from a point dipper.</p><p>When water waves pass from deep to shallow water they <b>slow down</b>, the <b>wavelength gets shorter</b>, but the <b>frequency stays the same</b> (it is set by the source).</p><h3>4. Sound</h3>\n<p>Sound is a <b>longitudinal</b> wave. It needs a medium and <b>cannot travel through a vacuum</b>. It travels fastest in solids and slowest in gases (about 340 m/s in air). Pitch depends on frequency, and loudness on amplitude. Humans hear about 20 Hz–20 000 Hz. <b>Ultrasound</b> (above 20 kHz) is used for baby scans and sonar.</p>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Echo example</b>A ship's sonar echo returns after 0.4 s, and sound travels at 1500 m/s in water. Depth = (1500 × 0.4)/2 = <b>300 m</b>. Halve it, because the sound goes down AND back up.</div><h3>5. Light is an electromagnetic wave</h3><p>Light is a <b>transverse electromagnetic (EM) wave</b>: it needs no medium and travels at 3 × 10⁸ m/s in a vacuum. Visible light is a small part of the EM spectrum.</p><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 80\" font-size=\"10\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"15\" width=\"46\" height=\"28\" fill=\"#c7d2e8\"/><text x=\"14\" y=\"58\">radio</text><rect x=\"56\" y=\"15\" width=\"46\" height=\"28\" fill=\"#b6c4e2\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"58\">micro</text><rect x=\"102\" y=\"15\" width=\"46\" height=\"28\" fill=\"#f3b9a8\"/><text x=\"106\" y=\"58\">infrared</text><rect x=\"148\" y=\"15\" width=\"46\" height=\"28\" fill=\"url(#g)\"/><text x=\"152\" y=\"58\">visible</text><rect x=\"194\" y=\"15\" width=\"46\" height=\"28\" fill=\"#c9b6ef\"/><text x=\"198\" y=\"58\">UV</text><rect x=\"240\" y=\"15\" width=\"46\" height=\"28\" fill=\"#a89cd6\"/><text x=\"244\" y=\"58\">X-ray</text><rect x=\"286\" y=\"15\" width=\"46\" height=\"28\" fill=\"#8a7cc2\"/><text x=\"290\" y=\"58\">gamma</text><defs><linearGradient id=\"g\"><stop offset=\"0\" stop-color=\"red\"/><stop offset=\".5\" stop-color=\"#3c3\"/><stop offset=\"1\" stop-color=\"violet\"/></linearGradient></defs><text x=\"10\" y=\"75\">long λ, low f, low energy  ⟶  short λ, high f, high energy</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The electromagnetic spectrum: all travel at 3 × 10⁸ m/s in a vacuum</div></div>", "quiz": [{"q": "A wave transfers…", "o": ["matter", "energy", "mass", "air"], "a": 1, "e": "Energy, not matter."}, {"q": "Waves entering shallow water…", "o": ["speed up", "slow down and the wavelength shortens", "stop", "increase in frequency"], "a": 1, "e": "Frequency stays the same; v and λ decrease."}, {"q": "v = fλ. f = 50 Hz and λ = 2 m. v =", "o": ["25 m/s", "100 m/s", "52 m/s", "0.04 m/s"], "a": 1, "e": "50 × 2."}, {"q": "Sound travels faster in…", "o": ["air", "a vacuum", "water", "space"], "a": 2, "e": "Particles are closer together; sound cannot travel in a vacuum."}, {"q": "In a longitudinal wave, particles vibrate…", "o": ["at 90° to the wave direction", "parallel to the wave direction", "in circles", "not at all"], "a": 1, "e": "Back and forth along the direction of travel, e.g. sound."}, {"q": "A wave has period 0.02 s. Its frequency is…", "o": ["0.02 Hz", "2 Hz", "50 Hz", "500 Hz"], "a": 2, "e": "f = 1/T = 1/0.02 = 50 Hz."}], "w": 3}, {"t": "Topic 3 – The wave nature of light", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain reflection and refraction using wave speed</li><li>describe dispersion and the spectrum</li><li>compare the particle and wave models of light</li><li>describe the modern wave–particle theory</li></ul></div><h3>1. Refraction explained by waves</h3><p>When light (or a water wave) enters a medium where it travels <b>slower</b>, the part of the wavefront that enters first slows down first. The wavefront swings round, so the direction changes. This is why light bends <b>towards the normal</b> in glass.</p><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 170\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"95\" width=\"320\" height=\"75\" fill=\"#dbeafe\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"11\">fast (deep / air)</text><text x=\"5\" y=\"165\" font-size=\"11\">slow (shallow / glass)</text><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"110\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"218\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"206\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"236\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"224\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"254\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"242\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"272\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"95\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"165\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"235\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"11\">wavefronts closer</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"153\" font-size=\"11\">→ ray bends</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Wavefronts slow down and bunch together, so the direction of travel (the ray) bends: this is refraction</div></div><div class=\"key\">n = v₁/v₂ = λ₁/λ₂ = sin θ₁/sin θ₂     (frequency does NOT change)</div><p>Reflection: the wave bounces off a barrier with its speed unchanged, so the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.</p><h3>2. Dispersion</h3>\n<p>A prism splits white light into a <b>spectrum</b>: Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo, Violet (ROYGBIV). <b>Violet bends most</b> (highest frequency, shortest wavelength) and <b>red bends least</b>.</p><h3>3. Models of light</h3><table><tr><th>Particle (Newton)</th><th>Wave (Huygens, Young)</th></tr><tr><td>Light is a stream of tiny particles (\"corpuscles\")</td><td>Light is a wave</td></tr><tr><td>Explains straight-line travel and reflection</td><td>Explains reflection, refraction, <b>diffraction and interference</b></td></tr><tr><td>Wrongly predicted light goes <i>faster</i> in water</td><td>Correctly predicts light goes <b>slower</b> in water</td></tr></table><p>Young's double-slit experiment (1801) showed interference fringes, which only waves can make, so the wave model won.</p><h3>4. The wave–particle theory today</h3><p>Today physicists accept that light behaves as <b>both</b>: it travels as a wave (diffraction, interference), but it gives up its energy in small packets called <b>photons</b> (e.g. in solar cells and the photoelectric effect). Each photon has energy E = hf, so blue light photons carry more energy than red.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Which colour bends most in a prism?", "o": ["Red", "Yellow", "Green", "Violet/blue"], "a": 3, "e": "It has the shortest wavelength, slowest speed and highest refractive index."}, {"q": "Dispersion happens because colours…", "o": ["have the same speed in glass", "have different speeds in glass", "are reflected", "are absorbed"], "a": 1, "e": "Different speeds mean different refractive indices."}, {"q": "When light enters glass, which does NOT change?", "o": ["speed", "wavelength", "frequency", "direction (unless along the normal)"], "a": 2, "e": "Frequency is fixed by the source."}, {"q": "Which observation supports the WAVE model of light?", "o": ["Shadows", "Interference fringes", "Reflection only", "Light travels in straight lines"], "a": 1, "e": "Only waves interfere."}, {"q": "Light travels at 2 × 10⁸ m/s in a glass. n =", "o": ["0.67", "1.5", "2", "6"], "a": 1, "e": "n = 3×10⁸ / 2×10⁸ = 1.5."}], "w": 4}, {"t": "Topic 4 – Diffraction and interference of light waves", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe superposition of pulses and waves</li><li>describe diffraction and how it depends on gap width</li><li>explain constructive and destructive interference</li><li>use λ = dΔx/L to estimate the wavelength of light</li></ul></div><h3>1. Superposition</h3><p>When two waves or pulses meet, the total displacement at any point is the <b>sum</b> of the separate displacements. After they pass, each continues unchanged.</p><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 120\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#bbb\"/><path d=\"M20 40 L40 40 L40 15 L70 15 L70 40 L110 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M210 40 L240 40 L240 20 L270 20 L270 40 L310 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"30\">→   ←</text><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#bbb\"/><path d=\"M120 100 L150 100 L150 55 L180 55 L180 100 L220 100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"70\">overlap: heights ADD</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Superposition: when two pulses overlap, the displacement is the sum of the two</div></div><h3>2. Wave behaviour</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Reflection</b>: bouncing off a barrier (an echo).</li><li><b>Refraction</b>: change of speed and direction (water waves slow down in shallow water).</li><li><b>Diffraction</b>: spreading out after passing through a gap or round an edge. It is greatest when the gap ≈ the wavelength.</li><li><b>Interference</b>: two waves meeting add together (superposition).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 130\"><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"50\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"20\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"45\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"70\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"95\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 40 A25 25 0 0 1 150 90\" transform=\"translate(0)\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 15 A50 50 0 0 1 150 115\" transform=\"translate(0)\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 -10 A75 75 0 0 1 150 140\" transform=\"translate(0)\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 -35 A100 100 0 0 1 150 165\" transform=\"translate(0)\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 40 A25 25 0 0 1 150 90\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 15 A50 50 0 0 1 150 115\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 -10 A75 75 0 0 1 150 140\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><path d=\"M150 -35 A100 100 0 0 1 150 165\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"11\">plane waves → gap → circular waves</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Diffraction: the waves spread out after the gap (the wavelength stays the same)</div></div>\n<h3>3. Interference and Young's double slit</h3>\n<p><b>Constructive</b>: crest meets crest, giving a bigger wave (a bright fringe). <b>Destructive</b>: crest meets trough, so they cancel (a dark fringe, a nodal line). The sources must be <b>coherent</b>: the same frequency and a constant phase difference.</p>\n<div class=\"key\">λ = d Δx / L<br>(d = slit separation, Δx = fringe spacing, L = slit-to-screen distance)</div>\n<div class=\"warn\">⚠ From the 1st to the 7th nodal line there are <b>6 spacings</b>, not 7. Convert all lengths to metres: 1 µm = 10⁻⁶ m, 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.</div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Path difference = 1.5λ gives…", "o": ["constructive", "destructive", "no interference", "diffraction"], "a": 1, "e": "Half-integer multiples of λ cancel."}, {"q": "Fringe width when λ = 600 nm, L = 2 m, d = 1 mm", "o": ["1.2 mm", "0.6 mm", "2.4 mm", "0.3 mm"], "a": 0, "e": "600e-9×2/1e-3 = 1.2e-3 m."}, {"q": "Diffraction is greatest when the gap is…", "o": ["much larger than λ", "about equal to λ", "zero", "curved"], "a": 1, "e": "Gap ≈ wavelength."}, {"q": "Bright fringes in Young's experiment are caused by…", "o": ["destructive interference", "constructive interference", "refraction", "reflection"], "a": 1, "e": "Crest meets crest."}, {"q": "Making the slits closer together (smaller d) makes the fringes…", "o": ["closer", "further apart", "disappear", "brighter only"], "a": 1, "e": "Δx = λL/d, so smaller d gives bigger Δx."}], "w": 6}, {"t": "Topic 5 – Kinematics: the description of motion", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>use displacement–time and velocity–time graphs</li><li>distinguish average and instantaneous velocity</li><li>use the equations of motion for constant acceleration</li></ul></div><h3>1. Key words</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Scalar (size only)</th><th>Vector (size + direction)</th></tr><tr><td>distance, speed, time, mass, energy</td><td>displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><b>Displacement</b> = the straight-line distance from start to finish, with its direction.</li><li><b>Velocity</b> = displacement ÷ time. <b>Acceleration</b> = change in velocity ÷ time (m/s²).</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Motion graphs</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 360 160\"><g font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"15\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"20\">s</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"145\">t</text><polyline points=\"30,130 80,70 120,70 165,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"95\" fill=\"#c0392b\">constant v</text><text x=\"82\" y=\"62\" fill=\"#c0392b\">at rest</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"155\">Displacement–time</text>\n<line x1=\"210\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"350\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"15\" stroke=\"#000\"/><text x=\"190\" y=\"20\">v</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"145\">t</text><polygon points=\"210,130 260,60 310,60 345,130\" fill=\"#d4a72c33\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"214\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#c0392b\">accelerating</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"55\" fill=\"#c0392b\">constant v</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"110\">area = distance</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"155\">Velocity–time</text></g></svg></div>\n<div class=\"key\">s–t graph: gradient = velocity<br>v–t graph: gradient = acceleration, AREA = displacement</div>\n<h3>3. Average and instantaneous velocity</h3><p><b>Average velocity</b> = total displacement ÷ total time. <b>Instantaneous velocity</b> = velocity at one moment, found from the <b>gradient of the tangent</b> to an s–t graph at that time (or a speedometer reading).</p><h3>4. Equations of motion (constant acceleration)</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">v = u + at<br>s = ut + ½at²<br>v² = u² + 2as<br>s = ½(u + v)t</div>\n<p>u = initial velocity, v = final velocity, a = acceleration, s = displacement, t = time. Write down what you know (e.g. u = 0, a = 3.2, t = 8, s = ?), then choose the equation that has those letters.</p>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A plane starts from rest and accelerates at 3.2 m/s² for 8 s. v = 0 + 3.2 × 8 = <b>25.6 m/s</b>; s = ½ × 3.2 × 8² = <b>102.4 m</b>.</div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The gradient of a displacement–time graph is…", "o": ["acceleration", "velocity", "distance", "force"], "a": 1, "e": "Δs/Δt."}, {"q": "The area under a velocity–time graph gives…", "o": ["acceleration", "displacement", "force", "speed"], "a": 1, "e": "v × t."}, {"q": "A car goes from 0 to 20 m/s in 4 s. a =", "o": ["5 m/s²", "80 m/s²", "16 m/s²", "0.2 m/s²"], "a": 0, "e": "(20−0)/4."}, {"q": "A runner goes 100 m east then 100 m back west in 40 s. Average velocity =", "o": ["5 m/s", "0 m/s", "2.5 m/s", "10 m/s"], "a": 1, "e": "Displacement is zero, so average velocity is zero."}, {"q": "A car slows from 20 m/s to 0 in 5 s. Acceleration =", "o": ["4 m/s²", "−4 m/s²", "100 m/s²", "−100 m/s²"], "a": 1, "e": "(0 − 20)/5 = −4 m/s² (deceleration)."}], "w": 7}, {"t": "Topic 6 – Vectors and scalars", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>add and subtract vectors by scale drawing and by calculation</li><li>resolve a vector into x and y components</li><li>give positions on an x–y plane</li></ul></div><h3>1. Key words</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Scalar (size only)</th><th>Vector (size + direction)</th></tr><tr><td>distance, speed, time, mass, energy</td><td>displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><b>Displacement</b> = the straight-line distance from start to finish, with its direction.</li><li><b>Velocity</b> = displacement ÷ time. <b>Acceleration</b> = change in velocity ÷ time (m/s²).</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Adding vectors</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 150\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"230,130 218,124 218,136\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"230,30 224,42 236,42\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"230\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><polygon points=\"230,30 216,32 222,42\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"148\" font-size=\"12\">4 km east</text><text x=\"236\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"12\">3 km north</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"#c0392b\">R = 5 km</text><path d=\"M70 130 A40 40 0 0 0 66 112\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"74\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"11\">θ</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Head-to-tail: R = √(4² + 3²) = 5 km, θ = tan⁻¹(3/4) = 37° north of east</div></div>\n<p>To <b>subtract</b> a vector (A − B), reverse B and add it: A + (−B).</p>\n<p>Components: a vector F at angle θ to the horizontal has F<sub>x</sub> = F cos θ and F<sub>y</sub> = F sin θ.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Which is a vector?", "o": ["Mass", "Speed", "Velocity", "Energy"], "a": 2, "e": "Velocity has a direction."}, {"q": "Resultant of 3 N east and 4 N north", "o": ["7 N", "5 N", "1 N", "12 N"], "a": 1, "e": "√(9+16) = 5."}, {"q": "Components of 10 N at 30° to the horizontal: horizontal =", "o": ["5 N", "8.7 N", "10 N", "17 N"], "a": 1, "e": "10 cos 30° = 8.7 N."}, {"q": "To find A − B you…", "o": ["add A and B", "reverse B and add it to A", "multiply them", "subtract their sizes only"], "a": 1, "e": "A − B = A + (−B)."}, {"q": "Which is a scalar?", "o": ["force", "displacement", "time", "acceleration"], "a": 2, "e": "Time has no direction."}], "w": 3}, {"t": "Topic 7 – Dynamics: the causes of motion", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe force as a vector and friction as an opposing force</li><li>use F = ma and the units newton and kilogram</li><li>calculate torque τ = Fd and use the balance of moments</li><li>find the vector sum of forces, including equilibrium</li></ul></div><h3>1. Newton's three laws</h3>\n<ol><li><b>First law (inertia)</b>: an object stays at rest or keeps moving at a constant velocity unless an <b>unbalanced force</b> acts on it. (That is why you need a seatbelt.)</li><li><b>Second law</b>: F<sub>net</sub> = m a. The bigger the net force, the bigger the acceleration; the bigger the mass, the smaller the acceleration.</li><li><b>Third law</b>: for every action there is an <b>equal and opposite reaction</b> on the <i>other</i> object. (A rocket pushes gas down, and the gas pushes the rocket up.)</li></ol>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 180\"><rect x=\"110\" y=\"70\" width=\"100\" height=\"45\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"97\" font-size=\"12\">plane</text><g stroke-width=\"3\"><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"15\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"115\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"170\" stroke=\"#7a4fc0\"/></g><text x=\"245\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">thrust</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"85\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#c0392b\">drag</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">lift</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#7a4fc0\">weight</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Free-body diagram. Net force = thrust − drag (if lift = weight).</div></div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Thrust 27 550 N, drag 7980 N, and the vertical forces balance. F<sub>net</sub> = 27 550 − 7980 = <b>19 570 N forward</b>.</div>\n<h3>2. Friction</h3><p>Friction is a force that <b>opposes motion</b> between surfaces in contact. It acts in the opposite direction to the motion (or attempted motion). It produces heat. It is useful (walking, brakes) and a nuisance (wears out engines – reduce it with oil or bearings).</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A 50 kg crate is pushed with 200 N against 80 N of friction. F<sub>net</sub> = 120 N, so a = 120/50 = <b>2.4 m/s²</b>.</div><h3>3. Torque (moment)</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">τ = F × d (N m)<br>d = perpendicular distance from the pivot</div>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 120\"><polygon points=\"160,80 145,110 175,110\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"72\" width=\"280\" height=\"8\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"25\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"11\">400 N</text><line x1=\"250\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"250\" y2=\"110\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"232\" y=\"118\" font-size=\"11\">W = ?</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"11\">1.8 m</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"11\">1.5 m</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Seesaw balance: clockwise moments = anticlockwise moments</div></div>\n<p>For balance: 400 × 1.8 = W × 1.5, so W = <b>480 N</b>. An object in <b>equilibrium</b> has no net force AND no net torque.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Net force = 0. The object…", "o": ["must be at rest", "is at rest or moving at constant velocity", "accelerates", "slows down"], "a": 1, "e": "Newton's first law."}, {"q": "The gradient of a Force vs acceleration graph equals…", "o": ["weight", "mass", "velocity", "g"], "a": 1, "e": "F = ma → m = F/a."}, {"q": "Unit of force", "o": ["kilogram", "newton", "joule", "watt"], "a": 1, "e": "1 N = 1 kg m/s²."}, {"q": "A 2 kg mass accelerates at 3 m/s². Net force =", "o": ["1.5 N", "5 N", "6 N", "0.67 N"], "a": 2, "e": "F = ma = 6 N."}, {"q": "Torque from 20 N acting 0.5 m from a pivot", "o": ["10 N m", "40 N m", "20.5 N m", "0.025 N m"], "a": 0, "e": "τ = Fd."}], "w": 5}, {"t": "Topic 8 – Momentum", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>use p = mv and impulse = FΔt = Δp</li><li>apply conservation of momentum in one and two dimensions</li><li>explain equal and opposite forces in collisions</li></ul></div><h3>1. Momentum and impulse</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">p = m v (kg m/s)<br>Impulse = F Δt = Δp = m v − m u</div>\n<p><b>Conservation of momentum</b>: in a closed system (no external force), the total momentum before = the total momentum after.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 90\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"45\" r=\"20\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"11\">2 kg</text><line x1=\"72\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"45\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"78\" y=\"38\" font-size=\"11\">3 m/s</text><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"45\" r=\"15\" fill=\"#9fb6e3\"/><text x=\"140\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"10\">1 kg</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"78\" font-size=\"10\">at rest</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"22\">→</text><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"45\" r=\"22\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><circle cx=\"278\" cy=\"45\" r=\"16\" fill=\"#9fb6e3\"/><text x=\"230\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"11\">stick together: v = ?</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Before: 2 × 3 = 6 kg m/s. After: 3 kg × v = 6 → v = 2 m/s</div></div>\n<p>Elastic collision: both KE and momentum are conserved. Inelastic: only momentum is conserved (some KE becomes heat or sound).</p>\n<div class=\"tip\">💡 Crumple zones, airbags and bending your knees when you land all <b>increase Δt</b>, so the force F = Δp/Δt is smaller.</div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Momentum of 3500 kg truck at 22 m/s", "o": ["77 000 kg m/s", "3522", "159", "7700"], "a": 0, "e": "3500 × 22."}, {"q": "Impulse equals…", "o": ["mass × acceleration", "change in momentum", "force × distance", "kinetic energy"], "a": 1, "e": "FΔt = Δp."}, {"q": "In an elastic collision, which is conserved?", "o": ["Only momentum", "Only KE", "Both momentum and KE", "Neither"], "a": 2, "e": "Elastic → KE conserved too."}, {"q": "A 0.5 kg ball hits a wall at 4 m/s and bounces back at 4 m/s. Change in momentum =", "o": ["0", "2 kg m/s", "4 kg m/s", "8 kg m/s"], "a": 2, "e": "Δp = 0.5×4 − (0.5×−4) = 4 kg m/s."}, {"q": "Airbags reduce the force on a driver by…", "o": ["reducing the change in momentum", "increasing the time of impact", "increasing speed", "reducing mass"], "a": 1, "e": "F = Δp/Δt."}], "w": 4}, {"t": "Topic 9 – Acceleration due to gravity", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe free fall with constant acceleration g</li><li>show that weight is proportional to mass (W = mg)</li><li>use a spring balance as a force meter</li></ul></div><h3>1. Free fall</h3><p>Without air resistance, all objects fall with the <b>same acceleration g</b>, whatever their mass. Near the Earth g ≈ 9.8 m/s² (KSSC uses <b>10 m/s²</b>). Use the equations of motion with a = g.</p><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 140\" font-size=\"11\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"10\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"15\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"30\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"55\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"90\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"135\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"20\">t = 0</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"135\">equal time intervals: gaps get bigger (speeding up)</text><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"215\" y=\"110\">v–t: gradient = g</text></svg></div><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A stone drops from a 45 m cliff. t = √(2h/g) = √(90/10) = <b>3.0 s</b>; v = gt = <b>30 m/s</b>.</div><h3>2. Mass and weight</h3>\n<p><b>Mass</b> (kg) = the amount of matter, a scalar, the same everywhere. <b>Weight</b> (N) = the force of gravity, a vector: <b>W = mg</b>. On the Moon your mass is the same but your weight is about 1/6.</p>\n<h3>3. The spring balance</h3><p>A spring balance (newton meter) measures force using Hooke's law: the spring stretches in proportion to the force. Hanging a mass on it reads its <b>weight</b> in newtons, e.g. 0.5 kg reads 5 N.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "In free fall (no air resistance), acceleration is…", "o": ["constant", "increasing", "dependent on mass", "zero"], "a": 0, "e": "g = 10 m/s² (KSSC value) for all masses."}, {"q": "Weight changes with location because…", "o": ["mass changes", "gravitational field strength changes", "volume changes", "density changes"], "a": 1, "e": "W = mg; g varies."}, {"q": "A 3 kg mass weighs (g = 10)", "o": ["3 N", "0.3 N", "30 N", "13 N"], "a": 2, "e": "W = mg."}, {"q": "A hammer and a feather dropped on the Moon (no air)…", "o": ["hammer lands first", "feather lands first", "land together", "float"], "a": 2, "e": "Same acceleration without air resistance."}, {"q": "Speed after falling from rest for 2 s (g = 10)", "o": ["5 m/s", "10 m/s", "20 m/s", "40 m/s"], "a": 2, "e": "v = gt."}], "w": 2}, {"t": "Topic 10 – Kinematics in two dimensions (projectiles)", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe projectile motion as a parabola</li><li>treat horizontal and vertical motion independently</li><li>calculate time of flight, range and maximum height</li></ul></div><h3>1. Projectiles</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 150\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000\"/><path d=\"M30 130 Q175 -60 320 130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"6,3\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#c0392b\">u at θ</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"11\">top: vertical v = 0</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"11\">range (horizontal distance)</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">The path is a parabola. Horizontal and vertical motions are independent.</div></div>\n<ul><li><b>Horizontal</b>: no force, so the velocity is constant: x = u cos θ × t.</li><li><b>Vertical</b>: acceleration g = 10 m/s² downwards (KSSC value). Vertical velocity = 0 at the top.</li><li>Time to the top = u sin θ / g. Total flight time = 2 × that (for level ground).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A ball is kicked at 32 m/s at 40°. uᵧ = 32 sin 40° = 20.6 m/s and uₓ = 32 cos 40° = 24.5 m/s. Time up = 20.6/10 = 2.06 s; total time = 4.11 s. Range = 24.5 × 4.11 = <b>101 m</b>.</div>\n<div class=\"warn\">⚠ For an object thrown horizontally, the impact speed is √(vₓ² + vᵧ²). Do not simply add the components.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "A ball is thrown horizontally. Its initial VERTICAL velocity is…", "o": ["the same as the horizontal", "0", "9.8 m/s", "unknown"], "a": 1, "e": "There is no vertical component at launch."}, {"q": "The horizontal velocity of a projectile (no air resistance)…", "o": ["increases", "decreases", "stays constant", "is zero"], "a": 2, "e": "No horizontal force."}, {"q": "At the top of its path, a projectile's vertical velocity is…", "o": ["maximum", "zero", "equal to g", "equal to its horizontal velocity"], "a": 1, "e": "It momentarily stops rising."}, {"q": "Ball rolls off a table at 4 m/s and lands after 0.5 s. Horizontal distance =", "o": ["0.5 m", "2 m", "4 m", "8 m"], "a": 1, "e": "x = 4 × 0.5."}], "w": 3}, {"t": "Topic 11 – Circular motion and gravitation", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain why circular motion at constant speed is accelerated</li><li>use v = 2πr/T, a = v²/r, F = mv²/r</li><li>use F = Gm₁m₂/r²</li><li>describe orbits of moons, planets and satellites</li></ul></div><h3>1. Circular motion and gravitation</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">v = 2πr/T<br>a = v²/r<br>F = mv²/r (towards the centre)<br>F = G m₁m₂/r²</div>\n<p>An object moving in a circle at constant speed is still <b>accelerating</b>, because its direction keeps changing. The net (centripetal) force points <b>towards the centre</b>. For a satellite, gravity provides this force.</p>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>The Moon: v = 1020 m/s and T = 27.3 days = 2.36 × 10⁶ s. r = vT/2π = 3.8 × 10⁸ m, and a = v²/r = 2.7 × 10⁻³ m/s².</div><h3>2. Orbits</h3><p>For a satellite, <b>gravity provides the centripetal force</b>: Gm<sub>E</sub>m/r² = mv²/r. So satellites further out move <b>more slowly</b> and take longer to orbit. A geostationary satellite orbits once every 24 h above the equator (used for TV and weather).</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Centripetal force points…", "o": ["outwards", "towards the centre", "along the tangent", "upwards"], "a": 1, "e": "Towards the centre of the circle."}, {"q": "Doubling the distance between two masses changes the gravitational force to…", "o": ["×2", "÷2", "÷4", "×4"], "a": 2, "e": "Inverse-square law."}, {"q": "A 2 kg mass moves at 3 m/s in a circle of radius 1.5 m. Centripetal force =", "o": ["4 N", "6 N", "12 N", "18 N"], "a": 2, "e": "mv²/r = 2×9/1.5 = 12 N."}, {"q": "If the centripetal force suddenly stops, the object moves…", "o": ["towards the centre", "outwards along the radius", "in a straight line along the tangent", "in a smaller circle"], "a": 2, "e": "Newton's first law."}], "w": 3}, {"t": "Topic 12 – Work and energy (including heat)", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>calculate work, power, kinetic and potential energy</li><li>use elastic PE = ½kx² and Hooke's law</li><li>apply conservation of energy</li><li>use Q = mcΔT and Q = mL</li></ul></div><h3>1. Work and energy</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">W = F d (J)<br>KE = ½ m v²<br>GPE = m g h<br>Power P = W/t (watts)</div>\n<p>Work is done when a force moves an object <b>in the direction of the force</b>. 1 joule = 1 N moving 1 m. Energy is the <b>ability to do work</b>. The area under a force–displacement graph = the work done.</p>\n<p><b>Conservation of energy</b>: energy cannot be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 150\"><path d=\"M20 30 Q120 30 160 130 Q200 30 300 30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"25\" cy=\"22\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"122\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"11\">GPE max, KE = 0</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"11\">KE max, GPE min</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"11\">GPE again</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A roller coaster or pendulum: GPE ↔ KE (total stays the same if there is no friction)</div></div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A 2 kg coconut falls 5 m. GPE = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100 J. Just before landing, KE = 100 J, so ½(2)v² = 100 → v = <b>10 m/s</b>.</div>\n<h3>2. Springs – Hooke's law</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 150\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"15\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M200 40 Q240 25 270 30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\"/><text x=\"195\" y=\"35\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#c0392b\">elastic limit</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"11\">F</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"11\">extension x</text><polygon points=\"40,130 200,40 200,130\" fill=\"#d4a72c33\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"11\">area = elastic PE</text><text x=\"85\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"11\">gradient = k</text></svg></div>\n<div class=\"key\">F = k x<br>Elastic PE = ½ k x²</div>\n<p>The extension is proportional to the force <b>up to the elastic limit</b>. k = the spring constant (N/m), found from the gradient. Remember: <b>extension = new length − original length</b>.</p>\n<h3>3. Heat and temperature</h3>\n<p>Temperature measures the <b>average kinetic energy</b> of the particles. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference.</p>\n<div class=\"key\">Q = m c ΔT (temperature change)<br>Q = m L (change of state, no temperature change)</div>\n<ul><li>c = specific heat capacity (water = 4200 J/kg°C, the energy to warm 1 kg by 1 °C).</li><li>L = latent heat (vaporisation of water = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 150\"><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"30\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><polyline points=\"30,120 70,95 130,95 160,50 250,50 290,20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"110\" font-size=\"10\">melting (0 °C)</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"10\">boiling (100 °C)</text><text x=\"5\" y=\"15\" font-size=\"11\">T</text><text x=\"230\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"11\">heat added</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Heating curve of ice → water → steam: the FLAT parts are changes of state</div></div>\n<p>During melting or boiling the temperature stays the same, because the energy is used to <b>break bonds</b> between the particles (latent heat), not to speed them up.</p>\n<p>Heat transfer: <b>conduction</b> (solids, particles passing on vibrations), <b>convection</b> (fluids, hot fluid rises), <b>radiation</b> (infrared, can cross a vacuum).</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "The area under a force–displacement graph is…", "o": ["power", "work done", "momentum", "velocity"], "a": 1, "e": "W = Fd."}, {"q": "During a change of state the temperature…", "o": ["rises", "falls", "stays constant", "doubles"], "a": 2, "e": "The energy goes into breaking bonds (latent heat)."}, {"q": "Elastic PE of a spring (k = 200 N/m) stretched 0.1 m", "o": ["1 J", "2 J", "10 J", "20 J"], "a": 0, "e": "½ × 200 × 0.01 = 1 J."}, {"q": "Heat to warm 2 kg of water by 10 °C (c = 4200)", "o": ["84 000 J", "8400 J", "42 000 J", "840 J"], "a": 0, "e": "2 × 4200 × 10."}, {"q": "Temperature measures…", "o": ["total energy", "average kinetic energy of particles", "heat", "mass"], "a": 1, "e": "Average KE."}, {"q": "KE of a 2 kg ball at 3 m/s", "o": ["9 J", "6 J", "18 J", "3 J"], "a": 0, "e": "½ × 2 × 9."}, {"q": "A 60 kg student climbs 5 m in 10 s. Power =", "o": ["30 W", "300 W", "3000 W", "50 W"], "a": 1, "e": "mgh/t = 60×10×5/10."}], "w": 10}, {"t": "Topic 13 – Ideal gas laws and kinetic theory", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>define pressure P = F/A</li><li>use the pressure–temperature and pressure–volume laws, and PV = nRT</li><li>convert between °C and K</li><li>link absolute temperature to average molecular KE</li></ul></div><h3>1. Pressure</h3><div class=\"key\">P = F/A   (pascal, Pa = N/m²)</div><p>The same force on a smaller area gives a bigger pressure (a sharp knife cuts, wide tyres don't sink in sand).</p><h3>2. Kinetic theory of gases</h3>\n<ul><li>A gas is made of tiny particles moving <b>randomly and quickly</b>, with large spaces between them.</li><li><b>Pressure</b> is caused by particles <b>colliding</b> with the container walls.</li><li><b>Temperature</b> measures the average kinetic energy. Hotter particles move faster and hit the walls harder and more often.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 130\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"15\" width=\"120\" height=\"100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"180\" y=\"15\" width=\"120\" height=\"100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"35\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"27\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"50\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"102\" y2=\"42\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"90\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"82\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"30\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"120\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"132\" y2=\"22\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"95\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"110\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"122\" y2=\"87\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"35\" cy=\"70\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><line x1=\"35\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"47\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"35\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"224\" y2=\"19\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"50\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"250\" y1=\"50\" x2=\"274\" y2=\"34\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"90\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"220\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"244\" y2=\"74\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"30\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"280\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"304\" y2=\"14\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"270\" cy=\"95\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"95\" x2=\"294\" y2=\"79\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><circle cx=\"195\" cy=\"70\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><line x1=\"195\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"219\" y2=\"54\" stroke=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"11\">cool: slow</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"11\">hot: fast → more pressure</text></svg></div>\n<h3>3. Absolute temperature</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">K = °C + 273<br>°C = K − 273<br>Absolute zero = 0 K = −273 °C</div>\n<p>At absolute zero the particles have the least possible energy. In the gas laws you must <b>always use kelvin</b>.</p>\n<h3>4. The gas laws</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Law</th><th>Constant</th><th>Relationship</th><th>Formula</th></tr><tr><td>Boyle</td><td>T</td><td>P ∝ 1/V</td><td>P₁V₁ = P₂V₂</td></tr><tr><td>Charles</td><td>P</td><td>V ∝ T</td><td>V₁/T₁ = V₂/T₂</td></tr><tr><td>Gay-Lussac (pressure)</td><td>V</td><td>P ∝ T</td><td>P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂</td></tr><tr><td>Combined</td><td>mass</td><td></td><td>P₁V₁/T₁ = P₂V₂/T₂</td></tr><tr><td>Ideal gas</td><td></td><td></td><td>PV = nRT</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 140\"><g font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><path d=\"M35 15 Q50 100 145 110\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"5\" y=\"15\">P</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"135\">V</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"40\">Boyle: P ∝ 1/V</text><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"10\" stroke=\"#000\"/><line x1=\"190\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"310\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><text x=\"172\" y=\"15\">V</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"135\">T (K)</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"40\">Charles: V ∝ T</text></g></svg></div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example 1</b>A gas at 180 kPa and 300 K is heated at constant volume to 420 kPa. T₂ = P₂T₁/P₁ = 420 × 300/180 = <b>700 K</b>.</div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example 2</b>n = PV/RT = (2.47 atm × 4.23 L)/(0.0821 × 290 K) = <b>0.44 mol</b>. Use R = 0.0821 L atm/mol K with litres and atm, or R = 8.31 J/mol K with m³ and Pa.</div>\n<div class=\"warn\">⚠ The most common mistake is forgetting to change °C to K. 27 °C = 300 K.</div>\n<p>Everyday examples: a tyre's pressure rises on a hot day (Gay-Lussac); a balloon shrinks in the fridge (Charles); a bubble grows as it rises in water (Boyle).</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "25 °C in kelvin", "o": ["298 K", "248 K", "25 K", "273 K"], "a": 0, "e": "25 + 273."}, {"q": "At constant temperature, if volume halves, pressure…", "o": ["halves", "doubles", "stays the same", "quadruples"], "a": 1, "e": "Boyle's law."}, {"q": "Gas pressure is caused by…", "o": ["gravity", "molecules colliding with the walls", "heat", "the container"], "a": 1, "e": "Kinetic theory."}, {"q": "In the gas laws, temperature must be in…", "o": ["°C", "°F", "kelvin", "joules"], "a": 2, "e": "Absolute temperature."}, {"q": "PV = nRT. What is n?", "o": ["number of moles", "pressure", "gas constant", "volume"], "a": 0, "e": "Amount of gas in moles."}, {"q": "Compressing air quickly makes it…", "o": ["colder", "hotter", "unchanged", "liquid"], "a": 1, "e": "Work done on the gas raises the molecules' KE."}], "w": 6}, {"t": "Topic 14 – Simple DC circuits and energy transfer", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe current as rate of flow of charge</li><li>use V = IR and resistance in series and parallel</li><li>describe how resistance changes with temperature</li><li>calculate power and energy in circuits</li></ul></div><h3>1. Charge, current and voltage</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">I = Q/t (A)<br>V = W/Q (V = J/C)<br>V = I R (Ohm's law)</div>\n<ul><li><b>Current</b>: the rate of flow of charge. <b>Conventional current</b> flows from + to −. Electrons flow from − to +.</li><li><b>Potential difference (voltage)</b>: the energy given to or used by each coulomb of charge.</li><li><b>Resistance</b>: opposition to current. Charges lose energy as heat. It depends on length (longer = more), thickness (thinner = more), material and temperature.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Circuit symbols</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 90\" font-size=\"10\"><g stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"18\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"42\"/><line x1=\"32\" y1=\"24\" x2=\"32\" y2=\"36\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><line x1=\"32\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"47\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"82\" y2=\"30\"/><rect x=\"82\" y=\"23\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\"/><line x1=\"112\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"124\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"145\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"155\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"167\" cy=\"30\" r=\"12\"/><line x1=\"159\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"38\"/><line x1=\"175\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"159\" y2=\"38\"/><line x1=\"179\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"189\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"220\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"232\" cy=\"30\" r=\"12\"/><line x1=\"244\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"254\" y2=\"30\"/><line x1=\"275\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"287\" y2=\"30\"/><circle cx=\"299\" cy=\"30\" r=\"12\"/><line x1=\"311\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"323\" y2=\"30\"/></g><text x=\"12\" y=\"62\">cell</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"62\">resistor</text><text x=\"155\" y=\"62\">lamp</text><text x=\"227\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"62\">ammeter</text><text x=\"294\" y=\"35\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\" font-weight=\"bold\">V</text><text x=\"276\" y=\"62\">voltmeter</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"82\">The ammeter goes in SERIES (low resistance). The voltmeter goes in PARALLEL (high resistance).</text></svg></div>\n<h3>3. Series and parallel</h3>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Series</th><th>Parallel</th></tr><tr><td>Current</td><td>The same everywhere</td><td>Splits: I = I₁ + I₂ + …</td></tr><tr><td>Voltage</td><td>Shared: V = V₁ + V₂ + …</td><td>The same across each branch</td></tr><tr><td>Resistance</td><td>R = R₁ + R₂ + …</td><td>1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + … (smaller than the smallest)</td></tr><tr><td>If one lamp breaks</td><td>All go out</td><td>The others stay on (house wiring)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 150\" font-size=\"11\"><g stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"300\" height=\"110\"/><rect x=\"40\" y=\"13\" width=\"40\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"100\"/><line x1=\"200\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"100\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"100\"/><line x1=\"130\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"20\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"13\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"53\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"93\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"250\" y=\"13\" width=\"40\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\"/><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"138\"/><line x1=\"168\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"168\" y2=\"134\" stroke-width=\"4\"/></g><text x=\"48\" y=\"10\">5 Ω</text><text x=\"140\" y=\"10\">2 Ω</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"57\">4 Ω</text><text x=\"185\" y=\"97\">6 Ω</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"10\">3 Ω</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"145\">12 V</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">2, 4, 6 Ω in parallel, in series with 5 Ω and 3 Ω</div></div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Parallel: 1/R = 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/6 = 11/12 → R = 1.09 Ω. Total = 5 + 1.09 + 3 = 9.1 Ω. I = 12/9.1 = <b>1.32 A</b>. V across the 3 Ω resistor = 1.32 × 3 = <b>4.0 V</b>.</div>\n<h3>4. Electrical power and energy</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">P = V I = I² R = V²/R<br>E = P t<br>Cost = kWh × price</div>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>A 1800 W iron draws 7.5 A. R = P/I² = 1800/56.25 = <b>32 Ω</b>. Used for 2 h: E = 1.8 kW × 2 h = 3.6 kWh.</div>\n<div class=\"tip\">💡 Safety: a <b>fuse</b> melts if the current is too large. The <b>earth wire</b> protects you if a metal case becomes live. Never touch switches with wet hands.</div><h3>5. Resistance and temperature</h3><p>In a <b>metal</b>, resistance <b>increases</b> as temperature rises (the ions vibrate more and get in the electrons' way). A filament lamp's I–V graph curves for this reason. A <b>thermistor</b>'s resistance <b>decreases</b> when it gets hotter.</p><p>The <b>e.m.f.</b> of a battery is the energy it gives each coulomb of charge. Charge is conserved: the current into a junction = the current out.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Two 4 Ω resistors in parallel", "o": ["8 Ω", "2 Ω", "4 Ω", "16 Ω"], "a": 1, "e": "1/R = 1/4 + 1/4."}, {"q": "Electrical power is…", "o": ["charge per time", "energy per time", "current per charge", "voltage per charge"], "a": 1, "e": "P = E/t (watts)."}, {"q": "I = 2 A, R = 10 Ω. V =", "o": ["5 V", "20 V", "12 V", "0.2 V"], "a": 1, "e": "V = IR."}, {"q": "Conventional current flows from…", "o": ["− to +", "+ to −", "north to south", "neutral to live"], "a": 1, "e": "Electrons flow the opposite way."}, {"q": "Metals conduct well because they have…", "o": ["many free electrons", "many protons", "no atoms", "heavy nuclei"], "a": 0, "e": "Delocalised electrons carry the charge."}, {"q": "Voltage is…", "o": ["energy per unit charge", "charge per second", "force per area", "current × time"], "a": 0, "e": "1 V = 1 J/C."}], "w": 10}, {"t": "Topic 15 – Magnetic forces and fields: the motor effect", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe magnetic fields and flux near magnets</li><li>use F = BIL and F = qvB</li><li>explain how an electric motor and a moving-coil meter work</li></ul></div><h3>1. Magnetic fields</h3>\n<p>Field lines go from <b>N to S</b> outside a magnet. Closer lines mean a stronger field. Like poles repel and unlike poles attract.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 330 150\"><rect x=\"120\" y=\"60\" width=\"45\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><rect x=\"165\" y=\"60\" width=\"45\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\">N</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fff\">S</text><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"><path d=\"M120 75 C80 49 250 49 210 75\"/><path d=\"M120 75 C80 101 250 101 210 75\"/><path d=\"M120 75 C60 23 270 23 210 75\"/><path d=\"M120 75 C60 127 270 127 210 75\"/><path d=\"M120 75 C40 -3 290 -3 210 75\"/><path d=\"M120 75 C40 153 290 153 210 75\"/></g><polygon points=\"165,49 159,45 159,53\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"11\">Field lines leave N and enter S</text></svg></div>\n<h3>2. The motor effect</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">F = B I L sin θ<br>(B in tesla, I in amps, L in metres)</div>\n<p>A current-carrying wire in a magnetic field feels a force at <b>right angles to both</b> the current and the field (right-hand slap rule: fingers = B, thumb = I, palm pushes = F). This is how <b>electric motors</b> and <b>loudspeakers</b> work.</p>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>L = 0.60 m, I = 0.050 A, B = 0.25 T. F = 0.25 × 0.050 × 0.60 = <b>7.5 × 10⁻³ N</b>.</div>\n<p>Two parallel wires: currents in the <b>same direction attract</b>, opposite directions repel. F = 2 × 10⁻⁷ I₁I₂L/r.</p>\n<h3>3. Moving charges, motors and meters</h3><div class=\"key\">F = q v B   (force on a charge moving at 90° to a field)</div><p><b>Electric motor</b>: a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field feels forces in opposite directions on its two sides, giving a <b>turning effect (torque)</b>. A <b>split-ring commutator</b> reverses the current every half turn so it keeps turning the same way.</p><p><b>Moving-coil meter</b>: the motor torque on the coil is balanced by a spring's opposing torque, so the needle's deflection is proportional to the current.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "A wire parallel to a magnetic field feels…", "o": ["maximum force", "zero force", "half force", "double force"], "a": 1, "e": "F = BIL sin θ, and θ = 0 gives F = 0."}, {"q": "F on a 0.8 m wire, 1.0 A, 0.5 T (perpendicular)", "o": ["0.2 N", "0.4 N", "0.8 N", "1.6 N"], "a": 1, "e": "0.5 × 1 × 0.8."}, {"q": "To make a motor spin faster you could…", "o": ["use a weaker magnet", "increase the current", "reverse the battery", "remove the commutator"], "a": 1, "e": "Bigger current → bigger force."}, {"q": "Force on an electron (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) at 2 × 10⁶ m/s across 0.5 T", "o": ["1.6 × 10⁻¹³ N", "3.2 × 10⁻¹³ N", "8 × 10⁻¹⁴ N", "1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ N"], "a": 0, "e": "qvB = 1.6e-19 × 2e6 × 0.5."}], "w": 4}, {"t": "Topic 16 – Magnetic fields from current-carrying conductors", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>draw the field around a long straight wire (right-hand grip rule)</li><li>describe the field between two parallel wires</li><li>state the direction of the force between parallel wires</li></ul></div><h3>1. Field around a current-carrying wire</h3>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 120\"><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"60\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"60\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"60\" r=\"28\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"60\" r=\"44\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"45\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"11\">⊙ out of page: ANTICLOCKWISE</text><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"60\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"232\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"248\" y2=\"68\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"248\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"232\" y2=\"68\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"60\" r=\"28\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"60\" r=\"44\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\"/><text x=\"200\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"11\">⊗ into page: CLOCKWISE</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Right-hand grip rule: thumb = current, curled fingers = field direction</div></div>\n<p>A dot ⊙ is the point of an arrow coming <b>towards</b> you (out of the page). A cross ⊗ is the tail feathers of an arrow going <b>away</b> (into the page).</p>\n<h3>2. Two parallel wires</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 90\" font-size=\"11\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"15\">I →</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"80\">I →</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"44\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">↓ attract ↑</text><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"180\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"15\">I →</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"80\">← I</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"44\" fill=\"#c0392b\">↑ repel ↓</text></svg></div><div class=\"key\">F = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × I₁ I₂ L / r</div><p>Currents in the <b>same direction attract</b>; opposite directions <b>repel</b>.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>Two 4.5 m wires 10 m apart each carry 2.75 A: F = 2×10⁻⁷ × 2.75 × 2.75 × 4.5/10 = <b>6.8 × 10⁻⁷ N</b>.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "A dot (•) on a wire means the current is…", "o": ["into the page", "out of the page", "zero", "alternating"], "a": 1, "e": "Like the tip of an arrow coming at you."}, {"q": "Two parallel wires carry current in opposite directions. They…", "o": ["attract", "repel", "no force", "twist"], "a": 1, "e": "Opposite currents repel."}, {"q": "A cross (⊗) on a wire means current is…", "o": ["out of the page", "into the page", "zero", "alternating"], "a": 1, "e": "Tail of the arrow."}, {"q": "The field lines around a straight wire are…", "o": ["straight lines", "concentric circles", "radial lines", "spirals"], "a": 1, "e": "Circles centred on the wire."}], "w": 3}, {"t": "Topic 17 – Electromagnetic induction", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe how a changing field or a moving conductor induces an emf</li><li>use emf = BLv</li><li>state Lenz's law</li><li>explain the transformer effect and use Vₛ/Vₚ = Nₛ/Nₚ</li></ul></div><h3>1. Electromagnetic induction</h3>\n<p>When a conductor <b>cuts through magnetic field lines</b> (or the field through a coil changes), a voltage (emf) is induced. If the circuit is complete, a current flows.</p>\n<div class=\"key\">emf = B L v<br>(wire moving at speed v)</div>\n<p>To get a bigger induced emf: move faster, use a stronger magnet, or use more turns on the coil. Reversing the motion or the field reverses the current.</p>\n<p><b>Lenz's law</b>: the induced current flows so as to <b>oppose the change</b> that caused it (conservation of energy).</p>\n<p>Uses: <b>generators</b> (power stations, bike dynamos) and <b>transformers</b>, where V<sub>s</sub>/V<sub>p</sub> = N<sub>s</sub>/N<sub>p</sub> (they only work with AC).</p><h3>2. Transformers</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 140\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"110\" y=\"20\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#555\" stroke-width=\"10\"/><path d=\"M95 35 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M95 49 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M95 63 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M95 77 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M95 91 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 28 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 37 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 46 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 55 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 64 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 73 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 82 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 91 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 100 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M195 109 q15 -6 30 0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"20\" y=\"75\">primary Nₚ</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"75\">secondary Nₛ</text><text x=\"120\" y=\"135\">soft-iron core</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Step-up transformer: more turns on the secondary → higher voltage</div></div><div class=\"key\">Vₛ/Vₚ = Nₛ/Nₚ     (ideal: Vₚ Iₚ = Vₛ Iₛ)</div><p>An alternating current in the primary coil makes a <b>changing</b> magnetic field in the iron core, which induces an alternating voltage in the secondary. Transformers only work with <b>AC</b>. Step-up: Nₛ > Nₚ. Step-down: Nₛ < Nₚ (e.g. phone chargers).</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>240 V to 12 V with 1000 primary turns: Nₛ = 1000 × 12/240 = <b>50 turns</b>.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "In Fleming's right-hand rule, the middle finger shows…", "o": ["motion", "field", "induced current", "force"], "a": 2, "e": "Thumb = motion, First = Field, seCond = Current."}, {"q": "Lenz's law says the induced current…", "o": ["helps the change", "opposes the change causing it", "is always zero", "flows clockwise"], "a": 1, "e": "This follows from conservation of energy."}, {"q": "ε = BLv. B = 0.5 T, L = 0.2 m, v = 4 m/s. ε =", "o": ["0.4 V", "4 V", "0.1 V", "1.6 V"], "a": 0, "e": "0.5 × 0.2 × 4."}, {"q": "A transformer will NOT work with…", "o": ["AC", "DC", "high voltages", "iron cores"], "a": 1, "e": "DC gives no changing field."}, {"q": "A transformer has 100 primary and 500 secondary turns. 12 V in gives…", "o": ["2.4 V", "60 V", "12 V", "600 V"], "a": 1, "e": "12 × 500/100."}], "w": 3}, {"t": "Topic 18 – Electrostatic fields and forces", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>explain charging by friction, contact and induction; use an electroscope</li><li>use Coulomb's law F = kq₁q₂/r²</li><li>define E = F/q and use E = V/d</li><li>link potential difference to energy: W = qV = ½mv²</li></ul></div><h3>1. Static electricity</h3>\n<p>Rubbing two materials transfers <b>electrons</b>. The object that gains electrons becomes <b>negative</b>, and the one that loses them becomes <b>positive</b>. Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. Only electrons move, never protons.</p>\n<p>Charging: by <b>friction</b> (rubbing), by <b>contact</b> (touching gives the same sign), or by <b>induction</b> (bringing a charge near, then earthing, gives the opposite sign).</p>\n<h3>2. The electroscope and conductors</h3><div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 150\" font-size=\"11\"><rect x=\"90\" y=\"40\" width=\"120\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"5\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"90\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"8\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#d4a72c\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"130\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"90\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"#888\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"165\" y=\"12\">metal cap</text><text x=\"175\" y=\"125\">gold leaves spread</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"80\">same charge on</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"93\">both leaves → repel</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">A gold-leaf electroscope detects charge</div></div><p><b>Conductors</b> (metals) let charge move; <b>insulators</b> (plastic, glass) do not. Charge is <b>conserved</b>: it is only moved, never made or destroyed.</p><p>Charging by <b>induction</b>: bring a charged rod near (charges separate), earth the far side with a finger, remove the finger, then the rod. The object is left with the <b>opposite</b> charge to the rod.</p><h3>3. Coulomb's law and electric fields</h3>\n<div class=\"key\">F = k q₁q₂ / r²  (k = 9 × 10⁹)<br>E = F/q<br>E = V/d (parallel plates)<br>W = qV = qEd</div>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 130\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><line x1=\"280\" y1=\"15\" x2=\"280\" y2=\"115\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#c0392b\">+</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"128\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">−</text><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"275\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><polygon points=\"165,30 157,26 157,34\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"275\" y2=\"55\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><polygon points=\"165,55 157,51 157,59\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"275\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><polygon points=\"165,80 157,76 157,84\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><line x1=\"45\" y1=\"105\" x2=\"275\" y2=\"105\" stroke=\"#0b1f4b\"/><polygon points=\"165,105 157,101 157,109\" fill=\"#0b1f4b\"/><text x=\"120\" y=\"10\" font-size=\"11\">uniform field: E = V/d</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">Between parallel plates the field is uniform, from + to −</div></div>\n<p>Like gravity, Coulomb's law is an <b>inverse-square law</b>: doubling the distance gives ¼ of the force.</p>\n<h3>4. Energy of a charge in a field</h3><div class=\"key\">W = qV      ½mv² = qV (charge accelerated from rest)</div><p>A charge accelerated through a p.d. changes potential energy into kinetic energy. 1 volt = 1 joule per coulomb.</p><div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>An electron (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C) accelerated through 100 V gains W = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100 = <b>1.6 × 10⁻¹⁷ J</b>.</div>", "quiz": [{"q": "Unit of electric charge", "o": ["ampere", "coulomb", "volt", "ohm"], "a": 1, "e": "Coulomb (C)."}, {"q": "Rubbing a balloon on hair transfers…", "o": ["protons", "neutrons", "electrons", "nuclei"], "a": 2, "e": "Only electrons move."}, {"q": "Force between two charges is F. Distance doubles. New force =", "o": ["2F", "F/2", "F/4", "4F"], "a": 2, "e": "Inverse-square law."}, {"q": "E between plates 0.02 m apart at 100 V", "o": ["2 V/m", "50 V/m", "5000 V/m", "2000 V/m"], "a": 2, "e": "E = V/d = 100/0.02."}, {"q": "Charging by induction leaves the object with…", "o": ["the same charge as the rod", "the opposite charge to the rod", "no charge", "protons only"], "a": 1, "e": "Opposite sign."}], "w": 5}, {"t": "Topic 19 – The atom and radioactivity", "notes": "<div class=\"tip\"><b>📋 Prescription – you should be able to:</b><ul><li>describe Rutherford's scattering experiment and the nuclear atom</li><li>explain isotopes and write decay equations</li><li>use half-life</li><li>describe effects, uses and safety of radiation, waste and fallout</li></ul></div><h3>1. The nuclear atom</h3>\n<p><b>Rutherford's gold-foil experiment</b>: most alpha particles went straight through, so the atom is mostly empty space. A few were deflected through large angles, so there is a tiny, dense, <b>positive nucleus</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 120\"><circle cx=\"150\" cy=\"60\" r=\"10\" fill=\"#c0392b\"/><text x=\"143\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\">+</text><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"20\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\"/><line x1=\"10\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\"/><path d=\"M10 55 L135 58 Q125 40 30 15\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M10 62 L138 61 L10 70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a4fc0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"180\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"10\">most pass straight through</text></svg></div>\n<p>Notation <sup>A</sup><sub>Z</sub>X: A = mass number (protons + neutrons), Z = atomic number (protons). Neutrons = A − Z. <b>Isotopes</b> have the same Z but a different number of neutrons.</p>\n<h3>2. Radioactivity</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>Charge</th><th>Stopped by</th><th>Ionising</th></tr><tr><td>Alpha α</td><td>Helium nucleus <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He</td><td>+2</td><td>Paper / skin</td><td>Very strong</td></tr><tr><td>Beta β</td><td>Fast electron <sup>0</sup><sub>−1</sub>e</td><td>−1</td><td>A few mm of aluminium</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Gamma γ</td><td>EM wave</td><td>0</td><td>Thick lead / concrete</td><td>Weak</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 130\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"50\" width=\"30\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#555\"/><text x=\"12\" y=\"95\" font-size=\"10\">source</text><path d=\"M40 60 Q120 55 200 20\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#c0392b\">α (bends a little)</text><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"65\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#1f8a4c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"60\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#1f8a4c\">γ (straight)</text><path d=\"M40 70 Q90 80 140 125\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3b7dd8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#3b7dd8\">β (bends a lot, opposite way)</text></svg><div class=\"cap\">In a magnetic or electric field</div></div>\n<h4>Decay equations (the numbers must balance)</h4>\n<p>Alpha: <sup>238</sup><sub>92</sub>U → <sup>234</sup><sub>90</sub>Th + <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He   (A − 4, Z − 2)<br>Beta: <sup>137</sup><sub>55</sub>Cs → <sup>137</sup><sub>56</sub>Ba + <sup>0</sup><sub>−1</sub>e   (A same, Z + 1)</p>\n<h3>3. Half-life</h3>\n<p>Half-life = the time for <b>half</b> of the radioactive nuclei to decay (or for the activity to halve).</p>\n<div class=\"ex\"><b class=\"t\">Worked example</b>100 mg, half-life 8 h. After 24 h (3 half-lives): 100 → 50 → 25 → <b>12.5 mg</b>. To reach 6.25 mg takes 4 half-lives = <b>32 h</b>.</div>\n<p>Uses: carbon-14 dating, cancer radiotherapy, tracers in medicine, smoke detectors (alpha). Dangers: radiation damages cells and DNA, which can cause cancer. Keep your distance, limit time, and use shielding.</p><h3>4. Radioactive waste and fallout</h3><p>Nuclear power stations and nuclear tests produce <b>radioactive waste</b> that stays dangerous for thousands of years; it must be sealed and stored deep underground. <b>Fallout</b> is radioactive dust spread by nuclear explosions. Kiritimati (Christmas Island) was used for British and US nuclear tests in 1957–62, and many people there were exposed to radiation.</p>", "quiz": [{"q": "Isotopes have different numbers of…", "o": ["protons", "electrons", "neutrons", "atomic number"], "a": 2, "e": "Same element, different neutrons."}, {"q": "Most to least penetrating", "o": ["α > β > γ", "γ > β > α", "β > α > γ", "γ > α > β"], "a": 1, "e": "Gamma passes through the most."}, {"q": "In beta decay the atomic number…", "o": ["decreases by 2", "increases by 1", "stays the same", "decreases by 1"], "a": 1, "e": "A neutron becomes a proton."}, {"q": "Rutherford's experiment showed a dense…", "o": ["electron cloud", "positive nucleus", "neutron shell", "gas"], "a": 1, "e": "A small, dense, positive nucleus."}, {"q": "80 g of a source with half-life 2 days. Mass after 6 days?", "o": ["40 g", "20 g", "10 g", "5 g"], "a": 2, "e": "3 half-lives: 80 → 40 → 20 → 10."}], "w": 7}],
papers:[
{y:2025, src:'P2025/KSSC Physics 2025.pdf', note:'There is no official 2025 Physics marking scheme online. These solutions are worked out independently. g = 10 m/s², as on the KSSC Physics equations sheet.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Light (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1a i.',q:'Explain how shadows form in terms of rectilinear propagation. (2)',a:'Light travels in straight lines (rectilinear propagation). When an opaque object blocks the light, the rays cannot bend around it, so a dark region (shadow) forms behind the object where no light reaches.'},
{n:'1a ii.',q:'Complete a ray diagram to find the position of the image of the box in the plane mirror. (3)',a:'1. Draw the image of the box the same perpendicular distance BELOW the mirror as the box is above it.\n2. Draw a straight (dashed) line from the image to the observer\'s eye. Where it crosses the mirror is the point of reflection; it must pass around the barrier.\n3. Draw the solid ray from the box to that point on the mirror, then to the eye, with arrows. Angle of incidence = angle of reflection.'},
{n:'1a iii.',q:'What does lateral inversion mean? (1)',a:'The image is reversed from left to right (e.g. your right hand appears as the left hand in the mirror).'},
{n:'1a iv.',q:'Two differences between real and virtual images in concave mirrors. (2)',a:'• A real image is formed where reflected rays actually meet and can be shown on a screen. A virtual image is where rays only appear to come from and cannot be shown on a screen.\n• A real image is inverted (object beyond F). A virtual image is upright and magnified (object between F and the mirror).'},
{n:'1b i.',q:'hₒ = 2.25 mm, u = 8.50 cm, convex lens f = 5.50 cm. Image distance? (3)',a:'1/f = 1/u + 1/v → 1/v = 1/5.50 − 1/8.50\n1/v = (8.50 − 5.50)/(5.50 × 8.50) = 3/46.75\n<b>v = 15.6 cm</b> (on the other side of the lens)'},
{n:'1b ii.',q:'Height of the image. (2)',a:'m = v/u = 15.58/8.50 = 1.83\nhᵢ = m × hₒ = 1.83 × 2.25 = <b>4.12 mm</b> (inverted)'},
{n:'1b iii.',q:'Nature of the image. (1)',a:'<b>Real</b> (and inverted, magnified).'},
{n:'1b iv.',q:'The convex lens is replaced with a concave lens. How does the image distance change? (2)',a:'The image distance <b>decreases</b>: the image forms on the same side as the object, between the lens and F, so it is virtual.\nConcave lens: 1/v = −1/5.5 − 1/8.5 → v = −3.34 cm (3.34 cm from the lens, virtual, upright, smaller).'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Refraction, waves and the nature of light (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'2a i.',q:'Two conditions for the critical angle (total internal reflection). (2)',a:'1. Light must travel from a denser medium to a less dense one (higher n to lower n).\n2. The angle of incidence must equal or exceed the critical angle (TIR happens when it is greater).'},
{n:'2a ii.',q:'Diamond to air, critical angle 24.4°. Refractive index of diamond? (2)',a:'n_diamond sin C = n_air sin 90°\nn = 1.00/sin 24.4° = 1/0.4131 = <b>2.42</b>'},
{n:'2b i–ii.',q:'Fill in: wave motion transfers ____; sound is a change in air ____.',a:'i. <b>energy</b>\nii. <b>pressure</b>'},
{n:'2b iii.',q:'Why does sound travel slower in air than in water? (2)',a:'In water the particles are much closer together and more strongly linked than in air, so each particle passes the vibration to the next one more quickly. In air the particles are far apart, so the vibrations are transferred more slowly.'},
{n:'2b iv.',q:'Show what happens to the wavefronts in shallow water. (2)',a:'Draw the wavefronts in shallow water <b>closer together</b> (shorter wavelength) and parallel. The waves slow down, so λ decreases while frequency stays the same. If they arrive at an angle, they also bend towards the normal.'},
{n:'2c i.',q:'What is the dual nature of light? (2)',a:'Light behaves both as a <b>wave</b> (it shows interference, diffraction and polarisation) and as a <b>particle</b> (photons, as in the photoelectric effect).'},
{n:'2c ii.',q:'Two failures of the corpuscular (particle) model. (2)',a:'1. It could not explain interference and diffraction (e.g. the bright and dark fringes in Young\'s experiment).\n2. It predicted that light travels FASTER in water than in air, but experiments show it is slower.'},
{n:'2c iii.',q:'Which colour bends more, red or blue? Why? (2)',a:'<b>Blue bends more.</b> Blue light has a shorter wavelength and travels more slowly in glass than red, so glass has a higher refractive index for blue and blue is refracted more.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Superposition, Young\'s experiment, motion graphs (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'3a i–ii.',q:'Waves A and E (same shape, crest then trough) fully overlap. Draw the resultant and name the interference.',a:'Both waves start with a crest followed by a trough (in phase), so the displacements add. Draw ONE wave of the same wavelength with <b>double the amplitude</b>.\nType: <b>constructive interference</b>.'},
{n:'3a iii.',q:'Two situations where diffraction is likely. (2)',a:'1. Waves passing through a gap about the same size as their wavelength (e.g. sea waves through a gap in a reef or harbour wall).\n2. Waves passing the edge of an obstacle (e.g. hearing sound around a corner of a building).'},
{n:'3a iv.',q:'Phenomenon when two diffracted waves meet. (1)',a:'<b>Interference</b>.'},
{n:'3b i.',q:'d = 2.50 mm, L = 3.25 m, λ = 450 nm. Fringe width? (2)',a:'Δy = λL/d = (450×10⁻⁹ × 3.25)/(2.50×10⁻³)\n= <b>5.85×10⁻⁴ m (0.585 mm)</b>'},
{n:'3b ii.',q:'How do coherent sources form stable interference patterns? (2)',a:'Coherent sources have the same frequency (wavelength) and a constant phase difference. The path difference to each point on the screen therefore never changes, so bright fringes (constructive) and dark fringes (destructive) stay in fixed positions.'},
{n:'3c i.',q:'What does the gradient of a displacement–time graph represent? (1)',a:'<b>Velocity</b>.'},
{n:'3c ii.',q:'Type of motion between 0 s and 3 s. (1)',a:'<b>Constant (uniform) velocity</b>: a straight line from 0 to 12 m, i.e. 4 m/s away from the start.'},
{n:'3c iii.',q:'Average velocity from 0 to 9 s. (2)',a:'Displacement at 9 s = 12 m\nAverage velocity = 12 m/9 s = <b>1.33 m/s</b>'},
{n:'3c iv.',q:'Meaning of negative displacement and negative velocity. (2)',a:'Displacement: the object is on the opposite side of the starting/reference point (behind it).\nVelocity: the object is moving in the opposite (backward) direction.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Kinematics, vectors and Newton\'s laws (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'4a i.',q:'a = 3.20 m/s² for 8 s from rest. Take-off velocity? (2)',a:'v = u + at = 0 + 3.20 × 8 = <b>25.6 m/s</b>'},
{n:'4a ii.',q:'Displacement before take-off. (2)',a:'s = ut + ½at² = 0 + ½ × 3.20 × 8² = <b>102.4 m</b>'},
{n:'4b i.',q:'One scalar and one vector. (2)',a:'Scalar: mass (or speed, distance, time, energy)\nVector: velocity (or force, displacement, acceleration, momentum)'},
{n:'4b ii–iii.',q:'Add vectors I and N on the grid. Find the direction of R. (1 square = 1 cm)',a:'Reading the grid: I ≈ 2 squares right, 4 squares down; N ≈ 4 squares right, 3 squares up.\nDraw N starting from the head of I (head-to-tail). R goes from the tail of I to the head of N.\nR = (2 + 4) right, (−4 + 3) up = 6 right, 1 down\n|R| = √(6² + 1²) = 6.1 cm\nDirection: θ = tan⁻¹(1/6) = <b>9.5° below the horizontal (to the right)</b>', flag:'Check your own grid reading. The method (head-to-tail, then tan⁻¹ of the components) is what earns the marks.'},
{n:'4c i.',q:'Two states of motion when the net force is zero. (2)',a:'1. At rest (stationary).\n2. Moving at constant velocity (constant speed in a straight line).'},
{n:'4c ii.',q:'Gradient of the Force vs acceleration graph and its units. (2)',a:'Gradient = ΔF/Δa = mass. Units: N/(m s⁻²) = <b>kg</b>.\nMethod: choose two points on the line, e.g. gradient = F at 3.75 m/s² ÷ 3.75.', flag:'The force axis on the printed graph has NO numbers, so a numerical value cannot be read. Full marks should go to correct working that shows gradient = mass in kg.'},
{n:'4c iii.',q:'Two differences between weight and mass. (2)',a:'1. Mass is the amount of matter (kg). Weight is the gravitational force on it (N).\n2. Mass is the same everywhere. Weight changes with location (W = mg, and g varies).\n(Also: mass is a scalar, weight is a vector.)'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – Torque, momentum, projectiles (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'5a i.',q:'Weight of the boy, m₁ = 38 kg. (1)',a:'w₁ = mg = 38 × 10 = <b>380 N</b>'},
{n:'5a ii.',q:'Weight of the girl to balance (r₁ = 4.2 m, r₂ = 3.5 m). (3)',a:'Clockwise moment = anticlockwise moment\nw₁ × r₁ = w₂ × r₂\nw₂ = 380 × 4.2/3.5 = <b>456 N</b>'},
{n:'5b i.',q:'Momentum is always conserved in a ____ or ____. (2)',a:'<b>collision</b> or <b>explosion</b> (in a closed/isolated system).'},
{n:'5b ii.',q:'200 g ball at 20.5 m/s is hit back at 57.8 m/s. Change in momentum? (2)',a:'Take "towards the pitcher" as positive: u = −20.5 m/s, v = +57.8 m/s\nΔp = m(v − u) = 0.200 × (57.8 + 20.5) = <b>15.7 kg m/s</b> towards the pitcher'},
{n:'5b iii.',q:'Contact time 0.05 s. Force on the ball? (2)',a:'F = Δp/Δt = 15.66/0.05 = <b>313 N</b>'},
{n:'5c i.',q:'Vertical component of initial velocity (fired horizontally). (1)',a:'<b>0 m/s</b>'},
{n:'5c ii.',q:'Describe the motion as it moves horizontally into a vertical force field. (2)',a:'Horizontal velocity stays constant (1025 m/s), because there is no horizontal force (ignoring air resistance). Vertically it accelerates downwards at g = 10 m/s². The combined path is a curve (parabola).'},
{n:'5c iii.',q:'Time to hit the water (45 m cliff). (3)',a:'s = ut + ½gt², with u_vertical = 0\n45 = ½ × 10 × t²\nt = √(90/10) = <b>3.0 s</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Circular motion, gases (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'6a i.',q:'Direction of the centripetal force. (1)',a:'<b>Towards the centre</b> of the circle.'},
{n:'6a ii.',q:'State the Universal Law of Gravitation and one use. (2)',a:'Every particle of matter attracts every other particle with a force proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them: F = Gm₁m₂/r².\nUse: calculating the orbits of satellites and planets (or the mass of the Earth, or explaining tides).'},
{n:'6a iii.',q:'0.2 kg stone, r = 2.5 m, one revolution in 3 s. Centripetal force? (3)',a:'v = 2πr/T = 2π(2.5)/3 = 5.24 m/s\nF = mv²/r = 0.2 × 5.24²/2.5 = <b>2.19 N</b>'},
{n:'6b i.',q:'Fill in: pressure comes from the ____ of molecules; temperature measures average ____ energy.',a:'1. <b>collisions</b>\n2. <b>kinetic</b>'},
{n:'6b ii.',q:'Pressure–volume relationship at constant temperature. (2)',a:'Boyle\'s law: pressure is <b>inversely proportional</b> to volume (PV = constant). If the volume is halved, the pressure doubles, because molecules hit the walls more often.'},
{n:'6b iii.',q:'Why does air in a bicycle pump get hotter when compressed? (2)',a:'The moving piston does work on the gas. Molecules bounce off the inward-moving piston with greater speed, so their average kinetic energy increases. Temperature measures average kinetic energy, so the temperature rises.'},
{n:'6b iv.',q:'758 K = ? °C; 698 °C = ? K (2)',a:'758 − 273 = <b>485 °C</b>\n698 + 273 = <b>971 K</b>'},
{n:'6b v.',q:'Moles in 92 L at 2.5 atm and 295 K (R = 0.0821). (2)',a:'n = PV/RT = (2.5 × 92)/(0.0821 × 295) = 230/24.22 = <b>9.50 mol</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q7 – Work, energy, heat (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'7a i.',q:'The area under a force–displacement graph represents… (1)',a:'<b>Work done</b>.'},
{n:'7a ii.',q:'Relationship between work done and energy. (1)',a:'Work done = energy transferred (1 J of work transfers 1 J of energy).'},
{n:'7a iii.',q:'F = 18 N, d = 5 m, W = 40 J. Angle between force and displacement? (3)',a:'W = Fd cos θ → cos θ = 40/(18 × 5) = 0.444\nθ = cos⁻¹(0.444) = <b>63.6°</b>'},
{n:'7b i.',q:'Explain an elastic collision. (1)',a:'A collision in which the total kinetic energy is conserved (no KE is lost as heat or sound). Momentum is also conserved.'},
{n:'7b ii.',q:'Plot Force vs extension (0,0), (1.5 cm, 75 N), (3.0, 150), (4.5, 225).',a:'Plot the four points and draw a straight line through the origin. This shows F ∝ x (Hooke\'s law). Gradient = 75/1.5 = 50 N/cm = 5000 N/m.'},
{n:'7b iii.',q:'k = 50 N/m, stretched 5.0 cm. Elastic PE? (2)',a:'E = ½kx² = ½ × 50 × (0.050)² = <b>0.0625 J</b>', flag:'The table gives k = 5000 N/m (50 N per cm), not 50 N/m, so the question contradicts its own data. With the stated 50 N/m the answer is 0.0625 J. With the graph value (5000 N/m) it would be 6.25 J.'},
{n:'7c i.',q:'True or False: during a change of state there is no temperature change. (1)',a:'<b>True</b>. The energy (latent heat) is used to change the state, not to raise the temperature.'},
{n:'7c ii.',q:'Two differences between heat and temperature. (2)',a:'1. Heat is the energy transferred because of a temperature difference (J). Temperature measures how hot something is, i.e. the average kinetic energy of its particles (°C or K).\n2. Heat depends on the mass (a bigger object holds more), but temperature does not.'},
{n:'7c iii.',q:'750 g of lead at 23 °C absorbs 2860 J (c = 128). Final temperature? (3)',a:'Q = mcΔT → ΔT = 2860/(0.750 × 128) = 29.8 °C\nFinal temperature = 23 + 29.8 = <b>52.8 °C</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q8 – Electricity (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'8a i.',q:'Match Column A to Column B: 1. Current 2. Electron (2)',a:'1. Current → <b>B</b> (caused by the movement/flow of charge)\n2. Electron → <b>C</b> (the carrier of charge)'},
{n:'8a ii.',q:'T/F: 1. Electric charge flows from lower to higher potential. 2. Resistances oppose charge flow and receive energy from it. (2)',a:'1. <b>False</b>. Conventional (positive) charge flows from higher to lower potential. (Electrons, being negative, move from low to high, but the statement as written refers to electric charge/current.)\n2. <b>True</b>'},
{n:'8a iii.',q:'Difference between conventional current and electron flow. (2)',a:'Conventional current flows from the positive (+) terminal to the negative (−) terminal. Electron flow is the actual movement of electrons from negative (−) to positive (+), in the opposite direction.'},
{n:'8a iv.',q:'What makes some materials good conductors and others bad? (2)',a:'Good conductors (metals) have many free (delocalised) electrons that can move easily and carry charge. Bad conductors (insulators such as plastic and rubber) have electrons tightly bound to their atoms, so charge cannot flow.'},
{n:'8b i.',q:'Show that the total resistance is 8 Ω. (3)',a:'Three 6 Ω resistors in parallel: 1/R = 1/6 + 1/6 + 1/6 = 3/6 → R = 2 Ω\nIn series with the fourth 6 Ω: R_total = 2 + 6 = <b>8 Ω</b> ✓'},
{n:'8b ii.',q:'Ammeter reading. (1)',a:'I = V/R = 24/8 = <b>3.0 A</b>'},
{n:'8b iii.',q:'p.d. across the 6.0 Ω in series with the source. (2)',a:'V = IR = 3.0 × 6.0 = <b>18 V</b>'},
{n:'8b iv.',q:'Energy supplied in 3 s to that resistor. (2)',a:'E = VIt = 18 × 3.0 × 3 = <b>162 J</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q9 – Electromagnetism (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'9a i.',q:'Principle of the electric motor. (2)',a:'A current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field experiences a force (motor effect). In a coil, the current flows in opposite directions on the two sides, so the forces are opposite and produce a turning effect (torque). A commutator reverses the current every half turn to keep it rotating.'},
{n:'9a ii.',q:'Coil 6.0 × 8.0 cm, I = 7.5 A, B = 0.43 T. Maximum force on the 8.0 cm wire. (2)',a:'F = BIL = 0.43 × 7.5 × 0.080 = <b>0.258 N</b>'},
{n:'9b i.',q:'The conductor shows a dot. Current into or out of the page? (1)',a:'<b>Out of the page</b> (a dot = the tip of the arrow coming towards you).'},
{n:'9b ii.',q:'Draw one field line and its direction. (2)',a:'Draw a circle around the conductor with an arrow going <b>anticlockwise</b> (right-hand grip rule: thumb out of the page, fingers curl anticlockwise).'},
{n:'9b iii.',q:'I = 2.5 A, B = 2.5×10⁻⁵ T. Distance of point A? (3)',a:'B = μ₀I/(2πr) → r = μ₀I/(2πB)\nr = (4π×10⁻⁷ × 2.5)/(2π × 2.5×10⁻⁵) = <b>0.020 m (2.0 cm)</b>'},
{n:'9c i.',q:'In Fleming\'s right-hand rule, the middle finger represents… (1)',a:'The direction of the <b>induced current</b>.'},
{n:'9c ii.',q:'State Lenz\'s law. (2)',a:'The direction of an induced current (or e.m.f.) is always such that it <b>opposes the change</b> in magnetic flux that produced it.'},
{n:'9c iii.',q:'Rod 15 cm, B = 0.32 T, induced voltage 0.165 V. Speed? (3)',a:'ε = BLv → v = ε/(BL) = 0.165/(0.32 × 0.15) = <b>3.44 m/s</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q10 – Electrostatics, atom, radioactivity (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'10a i.',q:'Unit of charge. (1)',a:'<b>Coulomb (C)</b>'},
{n:'10a ii.',q:'Describe charging by rubbing. (2)',a:'When two different insulating materials are rubbed together, friction transfers <b>electrons</b> from one to the other. The material that gains electrons becomes negatively charged, and the one that loses electrons becomes equally positively charged. Protons do not move.'},
{n:'10a iii.',q:'E = 54 N/C at 0.22 m. Both charge and distance are doubled. New E? (3)',a:'E = kQ/r² → E_new = k(2Q)/(2r)² = (2/4) kQ/r² = ½E\nE_new = ½ × 54 = <b>27 N/C</b>'},
{n:'10b i.',q:'Brief description of Rutherford\'s model. (2)',a:'The atom has a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus at its centre, containing almost all of its mass, with electrons orbiting around it. Most of the atom is empty space.'},
{n:'10b ii.',q:'Deflections confirmed the presence of the dense… (1)',a:'<b>(positively charged) nucleus</b>'},
{n:'10c i.',q:'Complete: ⁵⁹₂₆Fe → ? Co + ⁰₋₁e (2)',a:'⁵⁹₂₆Fe → <b>⁵⁹₂₇Co</b> + ⁰₋₁e\n(Mass: 59 = 59 + 0. Charge: 26 = 27 + (−1).)'},
{n:'10c ii.',q:'Two harmful effects of radiation on humans. (2)',a:'1. Ionising radiation damages cells and DNA, which can cause cancer (e.g. leukaemia).\n2. High doses cause radiation sickness and burns, and damage to reproductive cells can cause birth defects in future children.'},
{n:'10c iii.',q:'100 mg of I-131 injected at 8 pm Tuesday; 50 mg left at 3 pm Thursday of the next week. Half-life? (3)',a:'100 mg → 50 mg is exactly ONE half-life.\nTime: Tuesday 8 pm → Thursday of the next week, 8 pm = 9 days; minus 5 hours (3 pm) = 8 days 19 h\n= 211 hours\nHalf-life ≈ <b>211 h ≈ 8.8 days</b> (the accepted value for I-131 is about 8 days)'}
]},
{t:'Section B – Multiple choice (20 × 2 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'What is partial reflection?',a:'<b>D</b>: some light is reflected and some is transmitted.'},
{n:'2.',q:'Air → water (1.33) at 20°. Angle of refraction?',a:'<b>A, 14.9°</b>: sin r = sin 20°/1.33 = 0.257.'},
{n:'3.',q:'What causes dispersion of white light?',a:'<b>C</b>: different colours have different speeds in a medium.'},
{n:'4.',q:'Path difference 1.5λ gives…',a:'<b>C</b>: destructive interference (a half-odd multiple of λ).'},
{n:'5.',q:'A van moves in a circle at constant speed. Average and instantaneous velocity?',a:'<b>B</b>: average velocity over a full circle is zero (zero displacement); instantaneous velocity is not zero (its direction keeps changing).'},
{n:'6.',q:'v–t graph: a rectangle above the axis and a triangle below. The SUM of the areas represents…',a:'<b>D</b>: total distance travelled (adding both areas as positive). (Taking the area below as negative would give the displacement.)'},
{n:'7.',q:'3500 kg truck with p = 77 000 kg m/s. Velocity?',a:'<b>C, 22.0 m/s</b>: v = p/m = 77 000/3500.'},
{n:'8.',q:'Acceleration of a free-falling body (no air resistance)?',a:'<b>A</b>: it is constant (g).'},
{n:'9.',q:'Why does weight change with location?',a:'<b>D</b>: the gravitational field strength changes.'},
{n:'10.',q:'Temperature during the change from liquid to gas?',a:'<b>A</b>: remains constant.'},
{n:'11.',q:'Heat to melt 500 g of ice at 0 °C (L = 3.34×10⁵ J/kg)?',a:'<b>D, 1.67×10⁵ J</b>: Q = 0.5 × 3.34×10⁵.'},
{n:'12.',q:'Oxygen at 29.0 °C, 3.05×10⁶ Pa, cooled to 4.00 °C. New pressure?',a:'P₂ = P₁ × T₂/T₁ = 3.05×10⁶ × 277.15/302.15 = <b>2.80×10⁶ Pa</b>', flag:'None of the printed options is correct. Option B (4.21×10⁵ Pa) is what you get by wrongly using °C (3.05×10⁶ × 4/29). Always use kelvin.'},
{n:'13.',q:'Electrical power is…',a:'<b>B</b>: energy (J) per unit time (s).'},
{n:'14.',q:'Kettle: I = 5.0 A, R = 48 Ω. Power?',a:'P = I²R = 25 × 48 = <b>1200 W</b>: option <b>D</b>.', flag:'Option D is printed as "1,200 J". The correct unit for power is W (watts).'},
{n:'15.',q:'A current-carrying wire parallel to a magnetic field feels…',a:'<b>C</b>: zero force.'},
{n:'16.',q:'0.8 m wire, 1.0 A, 0.5 T (perpendicular). Force?',a:'<b>B, 0.4 N</b>: F = BIL.'},
{n:'17.',q:'Why is the Earth considered negatively charged?',a:'<b>C</b>: it has an excess of electrons.'},
{n:'18.',q:'The distance between charges is doubled. Force becomes…',a:'<b>A</b>: divided by four (inverse square).'},
{n:'19.',q:'NOT a characteristic of isotopes?',a:'<b>A</b>: "same number of neutrons" is false; isotopes have different numbers of neutrons.'},
{n:'20.',q:'Penetrating power from most to least?',a:'<b>D</b>: Gamma > Beta > Alpha.'}
]}
]},
{y:2024, src:'P2024/2024 KSSC PHY Q&A final.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the official marking scheme. ⚠ marks the places where the MOE scheme or the paper is wrong. g = 10 m/s² (KSSC equations sheet) unless the question gives 9.8.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Mirrors and lenses (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1a(i).',q:'A concave mirror forms an image of a bottle on a screen. Real or virtual?',a:'<b>Real</b>. Only a real image can be caught on a screen, because the light rays actually meet there.'},
{n:'1a(ii).',q:'v = 16.0 cm and f = 9.00 cm. Find the object distance u.',a:'Mirror formula: 1/f = 1/u + 1/v\n1/u = 1/9 − 1/16 = (16 − 9)/144 = 7/144\n<b>u = 144/7 ≈ 20.6 cm</b>'},
{n:'1a(iii).',q:'Image height 0.75 cm. Show the bottle is 0.97 cm tall.',a:'Magnification: hᵢ/hₒ = v/u\nhₒ = hᵢ × u/v = 0.75 × 20.6/16 = <b>0.97 cm</b>'},
{n:'1a(iv).',q:'Why can no image be obtained on a screen with a convex mirror?',a:'A convex mirror makes reflected rays <b>spread out (diverge)</b>. They never actually meet in front of the mirror, so the image is <b>virtual</b> (behind the mirror). A virtual image cannot be formed on a screen.'},
{n:'1b(i).',q:'Object between F and a convex lens: draw rays to locate the image.',a:'Ray 1: from the top of the object parallel to the axis → refracts through F on the far side.\nRay 2: from the top of the object straight through the centre of the lens (no bending).\nThese rays spread out on the far side. Extend them backwards (dashed lines) on the object side: they meet <b>above and behind the object</b>. That point is the top of the image IC.'},
{n:'1b(ii).',q:'Nature, orientation and size of the image',a:'<b>Virtual, erect (upright), enlarged</b>. This is how a magnifying glass works.'},
{n:'1b(iii).',q:'Camera image vs magnifying-glass image',a:'Camera: the object is far away (beyond 2F), so the image is <b>real, inverted and diminished</b> and falls on the film or sensor.\nMagnifying glass: the object is closer than F, so the image is <b>virtual, upright and enlarged</b>.'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Refraction and waves (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'2a(i).',q:'Draw incident and refracted rays as light goes from seawater (n = 1.33) into air',a:'Draw the incident ray in the water going up to the surface at an angle to the normal. In the air, the refracted ray bends <b>away from the normal</b> (a bigger angle), because light speeds up going into a less dense medium. Add arrows on both rays.'},
{n:'2a(ii).',q:'Name of the gradient of a sin θ₁ vs sin θ₂ graph',a:'The <b>refractive index</b> (Snell\'s law: n = sin θ₁ / sin θ₂).'},
{n:'2a(iii).',q:'Angle of refraction at the critical angle',a:'<b>90°</b>. The refracted ray runs along the boundary.'},
{n:'2b(i).',q:'Define wavelength and frequency',a:'<b>Wavelength (λ)</b>: the distance between two neighbouring points in phase, e.g. crest to crest (in m).\n<b>Frequency (f)</b>: the number of complete waves passing a point each second (in Hz).'},
{n:'2b(ii).',q:'Longitudinal wave: wave direction vs particle movement (with a diagram)',a:'In a longitudinal wave the particles vibrate <b>back and forth parallel to the direction the wave travels</b>, which makes compressions (particles close together) and rarefactions (particles spread out).\nDiagram: a spring or row of dots with bunched and spread regions. Put a ↔ arrow for the particle movement and a → arrow for the wave direction. Example: sound.'},
{n:'2b(iii).',q:'12 waves in 15 s, λ = 0.5 m. Show v = 0.4 m/s',a:'f = 12/15 = 0.8 Hz\nv = fλ = 0.8 × 0.5 = <b>0.4 m/s</b>'},
{n:'2c(i).',q:'Refraction is greatest in the (lower/higher) frequency range; red light bends (less/more) than blue',a:'<b>higher</b> frequency; red bends <b>less</b>.'},
{n:'2c(ii).',q:'Discuss the wave model of light. Give an example.',a:'The wave model says light travels as a <b>transverse electromagnetic wave</b>. So light can reflect, refract, <b>diffract</b> (spread round edges) and <b>interfere</b>, just like water waves.\nExample: in Young\'s double-slit experiment, light makes bright and dark fringes (interference). Only waves can do this.'},
{n:'2c(iii).',q:'Show what happens to diffracted waves after they pass boundary B',a:'When the waves pass through the gap in boundary B they <b>diffract again</b>: they spread out into curved (circular) wavefronts beyond the gap. Keep the <b>same wavelength</b> (same spacing). A narrower gap gives more spreading.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Interference and motion graphs (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'3a(i).',q:'Two square pulses fully overlap. Draw the resultant',a:'Superposition principle: <b>add the displacements</b>. Two upward pulses of heights h₁ and h₂ give one square pulse of height h₁ + h₂ (same width). If one pulse is up and one is down, subtract them.'},
{n:'3a(ii).',q:'Why can\'t two independent light sources show interference?',a:'Interference needs <b>coherent</b> sources: the same frequency and a <b>constant phase difference</b>. Two separate lamps send out light in random bursts, so their phase difference keeps changing and the fringes blur out. One source split by two slits is coherent.'},
{n:'3a(iii).',q:'T/F: 1. Destructive interference: peaks line up with troughs. 2. Constructive: peaks and troughs line up.',a:'1. <b>T</b>\n2. <b>F</b>. Constructive interference is peaks lining up with peaks (and troughs with troughs).'},
{n:'3b(i).',q:'1st to 7th nodal line = 6.0 cm, L = 2.5 m, d = 1.6 × 10² µm. Find λ',a:'From the 1st to the 7th nodal line there are <b>6 fringe spacings</b>: Δx = 6.0/6 = 1.0 cm = 0.010 m\nd = 1.6 × 10² µm = 1.6 × 10⁻⁴ m\nλ = dΔx/L = (1.6 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.010)/2.5 = <b>6.4 × 10⁻⁷ m (640 nm)</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme gives 6.4 × 10⁻⁹ m, which is a power-of-ten mistake. 640 nm is red/orange visible light.'},
{n:'3b(ii).',q:'Effect of a shorter wavelength on the pattern',a:'Δx = λL/d, so a shorter λ makes the fringes <b>closer together</b> (narrower pattern) when L and d stay the same.'},
{n:'3c(i).',q:'Gradient of a displacement–time graph',a:'<b>Velocity</b>.'},
{n:'3c(ii).',q:'Motion between 1445 and 1530',a:'The graph is flat, so she is <b>stationary (at rest)</b>: velocity = 0.'},
{n:'3c(iii).',q:'Distance from home to the airport',a:'<b>40 km</b>.'},
{n:'3c(iv).',q:'Minutes waiting at the airport',a:'1445 → 1530 = <b>45 minutes</b>.'},
{n:'3c(v).',q:'Average velocity home → airport (1400–1445) in m/s',a:'v = s/t = 40 000 m ÷ (45 × 60 s) = 40 000/2700 = <b>14.8 m/s</b> (towards the airport)'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Kinematics, vectors and Newton\'s laws (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'4a(i).',q:'0 → 5.0 m/s in 12 s. Acceleration?',a:'a = (v − u)/t = (5.0 − 0)/12 = <b>0.42 m/s²</b>'},
{n:'4a(ii).',q:'Show the displacement is 30 m',a:'s = ½(u + v)t = ½(0 + 5.0)(12) = <b>30 m</b>'},
{n:'4b(i).',q:'Scalar vs vector',a:'A <b>scalar</b> has size (magnitude) only. A <b>vector</b> has size <b>and direction</b>.'},
{n:'4b(ii).',q:'One example of each',a:'Scalar: mass, distance, speed, time, energy.\nVector: displacement, velocity, force, acceleration, momentum.'},
{n:'4b(iii).',q:'Draw R = M − N',a:'M − N = M + (−N). <b>Reverse the arrow of N</b> (same length, opposite direction). Join its tail to the head of M. The resultant R goes from the tail of M to the head of −N.'},
{n:'4c(i).',q:'What causes motion (a change in motion)?',a:'An <b>unbalanced (net) force</b>.'},
{n:'4c(ii).',q:'In F = ma, F stands for…',a:'The <b>net (unbalanced/resultant) force</b>, in newtons.'},
{n:'4c(iii).',q:'Two differences between mass and weight',a:'1. Mass is the <b>amount of matter</b> (kg). Weight is the <b>gravitational force</b> on it (N).\n2. Mass is a <b>scalar</b> and the same everywhere. Weight is a <b>vector</b> (points down) and changes with g, e.g. on the Moon.\nW = mg.'},
{n:'4c(iv).',q:'Thrust 27 550 N, drag 7980 N, weight 7700 N, two reaction forces 3850 N. Unbalanced force?',a:'Vertical: 3850 + 3850 = 7700 N up = weight 7700 N down → net 0\nHorizontal: 27 550 − 7980 = <b>19 570 N forward</b> (in the thrust direction)'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – Torque, momentum and projectiles (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'5a(i).',q:'Velocity and acceleration of a moving body in equilibrium',a:'The net force is zero, so the <b>acceleration is zero</b> and the body moves at a <b>constant velocity</b> (same speed, straight line).'},
{n:'5a(ii).',q:'Torque 31.5 N m from a force of 450 N. Show d = 0.07 m',a:'τ = Fd → d = τ/F = 31.5/450 = <b>0.07 m</b>'},
{n:'5b(i).',q:'Define momentum. Scalar or vector?',a:'Momentum = <b>mass × velocity</b> (p = mv, in kg m/s). It is a <b>vector</b> (same direction as the velocity).'},
{n:'5b(ii).',q:'A 0.65 kg ball falls for 1.4 s. Impulse?',a:'The only force is its weight: F = mg = 0.65 × 10 = 6.5 N\nImpulse = FΔt = 6.5 × 1.4 = <b>9.1 N s</b>'},
{n:'5b(iii).',q:'Why is the falling ball\'s momentum not conserved?',a:'Momentum is conserved only in an <b>isolated system</b> (no external force). The ball has an <b>external force</b> (gravity) acting on it, so its momentum keeps increasing. (For the ball + Earth together, momentum is conserved.)'},
{n:'5c(i).',q:'Sketch the path of the kicked ball',a:'A symmetric <b>parabola</b> (upside-down U) starting at the origin, rising to a maximum height, then coming back down to the x-axis.'},
{n:'5c(ii).',q:'u = 32 m/s at 40°. Find the vertical and horizontal components',a:'Vertical: 32 sin 40° = <b>20.6 m/s</b>\nHorizontal: 32 cos 40° = <b>24.5 m/s</b>'},
{n:'5c(iii).',q:'Total horizontal displacement',a:'Time to the top: t = 20.6/10 = 2.06 s, so the total time is 4.11 s\nRange = 24.5 × 4.11 = <b>≈ 101 m</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Gravitation and gases (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'6a(i).',q:'Two uses of the universal law of gravitation',a:'1. Calculating the <b>orbits of satellites and planets</b> (their speed, period and height).\n2. Explaining <b>tides</b>, which are caused by the Moon\'s and Sun\'s pull on the oceans.\n(It is also used to find the mass of the Earth or of planets.)'},
{n:'6a(ii).',q:'Moon: v = 1020 km/s and T = 27 days (1620 h). Find the orbit radius',a:'v = 2πr/T → r = vT/2π\nUsing the paper\'s numbers (v = 1.02 × 10⁶ m/s, T = 1620 h = 5.83 × 10⁶ s):\nr = (1.02 × 10⁶ × 5.83 × 10⁶)/2π = <b>9.47 × 10¹¹ m</b> (the MOE answer)\n⚠ The paper has two errors. 27 days is <b>648 h</b>, not 1620 h, and the Moon\'s real speed is <b>1020 m/s</b>, not km/s. With the true values: r = 1020 × 2.33 × 10⁶/2π ≈ <b>3.8 × 10⁸ m</b>, the real Earth–Moon distance.'},
{n:'6a(iii).',q:'Centripetal acceleration of the Moon',a:'a = v²/r\nPaper\'s numbers: (1.02 × 10⁶)²/9.47 × 10¹¹ = <b>1.1 m/s²</b> (MOE answer)\nTrue values: 1020²/3.8 × 10⁸ ≈ <b>2.7 × 10⁻³ m/s²</b>'},
{n:'6b(i).',q:'The forces that produce the ___ of a gas come from the ___ of moving molecules',a:'<b>pressure</b>; <b>collisions</b>.'},
{n:'6b(ii)1.',q:'Temperature in terms of energy and particles',a:'Temperature measures the <b>average kinetic energy</b> of the gas particles. Hotter means the particles move faster.'},
{n:'6b(ii)2.',q:'Pressure increased at constant volume and mass: what happens to T?',a:'<b>Temperature increases</b> (P ∝ T at constant V: Gay-Lussac\'s law).'},
{n:'6b(iii).',q:'Convert 796.2 °C to K, and 2118.8 K to °C',a:'K = °C + 273: 796.2 + 273 = <b>1069.2 K</b>\n°C = K − 273: 2118.8 − 273 = <b>1845.8 °C</b>'},
{n:'6b(iv).',q:'180 kPa at 300 K, constant volume, now 420 kPa. Show T₂ = 700 K',a:'P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂ → T₂ = P₂T₁/P₁ = 420 × 300/180 = <b>700 K</b>'},
{n:'6b(v).',q:'V = 4.23 L, P = 2.47 atm, T = 290 K, R = 0.0821. Find n',a:'n = PV/RT = (2.47 × 4.23)/(0.0821 × 290) = 10.45/23.81 = <b>0.44 mol</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q7 – Springs, work and heat (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'7a(i).',q:'Plot force vs extension',a:'Plot the points (5.2, 0), (6.4, 25), (7.6, 50), (8.8, 75) with extension (cm) on the x-axis and force (N) on the y-axis. Draw a straight line through them with a ruler.\n⚠ The table has an error: 250 g weighs 2.5 N, not 25 N. Use the values printed, as the marking scheme does.'},
{n:'7a(ii).',q:'What does the gradient give?',a:'The <b>spring constant k</b> (stiffness), from Hooke\'s law F = kx.'},
{n:'7a(iii).',q:'Calculate the gradient with units',a:'k = ΔF/Δx = (75 − 0)/(0.088 − 0.052) = 75/0.036 = <b>2083 N/m (≈ 2.1 × 10³ N/m)</b>'},
{n:'7a(iv).',q:'Elastic PE at 8.0 cm',a:'Eₚ = ½kx² = ½ × 2083 × (0.080)² = <b>6.67 J</b> (MOE answer)\nNote: the "extension" column really looks like the spring\'s length (5.20 cm at 0 N). If so, the true extension at 8.0 cm is 2.8 cm, giving ½ × 2083 × 0.028² = 0.82 J. In the exam, follow the question as printed: 6.67 J.'},
{n:'7b(i).',q:'Area under a force–displacement graph',a:'<b>Work done</b> (energy transferred), in joules.'},
{n:'7b(ii).',q:'What happens to energy when work is done on a body?',a:'Energy is <b>transferred</b> to the body (or changed from one form to another). The work done equals the energy transferred.'},
{n:'7b(iii).',q:'KE and momentum in an elastic collision',a:'Both <b>kinetic energy and momentum are conserved</b>. (In an inelastic collision only momentum is conserved.)'},
{n:'7b(iv).',q:'Why does the GPE of a stone thrown upward increase?',a:'GPE = mgh. As the stone rises its <b>height h increases</b>, so its GPE increases. Its kinetic energy is changing into GPE (it slows down).'},
{n:'7c(i).',q:'725 g heated from 20 °C to 100 °C uses 243 600 J. Show c = 4200',a:'ΔT = 100 − 20 = 80 °C\nc = Q/(mΔT) = 243 600/(0.725 × 80) = <b>4200 J/kg°C</b>'},
{n:'7c(ii).',q:'Energy to vaporise 85 g (L = 2.26 × 10⁶ J/kg)',a:'Q = mL = 0.085 × 2.26 × 10⁶ = <b>1.92 × 10⁵ J</b> (192 100 J)'},
{n:'7c(iii).',q:'Why doesn\'t the temperature change while boiling?',a:'The energy is used as <b>latent heat</b> to <b>break the bonds</b> between the water molecules (changing the state), not to increase their kinetic energy. So the temperature stays at 100 °C.'}
]},
{t:'Q8 – Electric current and circuits (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'8a(i).',q:'In an electric field, current results from…',a:'The <b>flow of charges</b> (electrons) pushed by the electric field (potential difference).'},
{n:'8a(ii).',q:'Flow of charge in terms of potential',a:'Positive charge flows from <b>high potential to low potential</b>.'},
{n:'8a(iii).',q:'Conventional current vs electron flow',a:'Conventional current: from the <b>positive (+) terminal to the negative (−)</b> terminal.\nElectron flow: the opposite way, <b>− to +</b>.'},
{n:'8a(iv).',q:'What does resistance do to the charges and their energy?',a:'Resistance <b>opposes the flow</b> of charges (slows them, reducing the current). The charges <b>lose energy</b>, which changes to <b>heat (and light)</b> in the resistor.'},
{n:'8b(i).',q:'Total resistance: 2 Ω, 4 Ω, 6 Ω in parallel, in series with 5 Ω and 3 Ω',a:'Parallel: 1/R = 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/6 = (6 + 3 + 2)/12 = 11/12 → R = 12/11 = 1.09 Ω\nTotal: Rₜ = 5 + 1.09 + 3 = <b>9.1 Ω</b>'},
{n:'8b(ii).',q:'Ammeter reading',a:'I = V/R = 12/9.1 = <b>1.3 A</b>'},
{n:'8b(iii).',q:'Show the p.d. across the 3.0 Ω resistor is 4.0 V',a:'V = IR = 1.32 × 3.0 = <b>4.0 V</b>'},
{n:'8c(i).',q:'Iron: 1800 W, 7.5 A. Resistance?',a:'P = I²R → R = P/I² = 1800/7.5² = 1800/56.25 = <b>32 Ω</b>'},
{n:'8c(ii).',q:'Fridge: 920 W, 240 V. Resistance?',a:'P = V²/R → R = V²/P = 240²/920 = 57 600/920 = <b>62.6 Ω</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q9 – Magnetism and induction (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'9a(i).',q:'Describe the motor effect',a:'A <b>current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field feels a force</b>. This happens because its own field interacts with the magnet\'s field. The force is at right angles to both the current and the field (use the right-hand / Fleming\'s left-hand rule).'},
{n:'9a(ii).',q:'L = 60 cm, I = 50 mA, B = 0.25 T. Show F = 7.5 × 10⁻³ N',a:'F = BIL = 0.25 × 0.050 × 0.60 = <b>7.5 × 10⁻³ N</b>'},
{n:'9b(i).',q:'Wire 1 shows ⊙ and Wire 2 shows ⊗. Which is into and which out of the page?',a:'Wire 1 (dot ⊙): <b>out of the page</b> (you see the arrow\'s point).\nWire 2 (cross ⊗): <b>into the page</b> (you see the arrow\'s tail feathers).'},
{n:'9b(ii).',q:'Draw field lines round Wire 2',a:'Draw <b>concentric circles</b> round the wire, spaced further apart as you go outwards. The current is into the page, so the arrows go <b>clockwise</b> (right-hand grip rule: thumb into the page, fingers curl clockwise).'},
{n:'9b(iii).',q:'Two parallel wires 4.50 m long, 2.75 A each, 10.0 m apart. Show F = 680 × 10⁻⁹ N',a:'F = k I₁I₂ L/r, with k = 2 × 10⁻⁷\nF = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × 2.75 × 2.75 × 4.50/10.0 = <b>6.8 × 10⁻⁷ N</b> = 680 × 10⁻⁹ N'},
{n:'9c(i).',q:'Effect on the induced current of: 1. reversing the field 2. cutting the field faster',a:'1. The current <b>reverses direction</b>.\n2. The current (emf) <b>increases</b>.'},
{n:'9c(ii).',q:'State Lenz\'s law',a:'The induced current flows in a direction that <b>opposes the change</b> that produced it. (This follows from conservation of energy.)'},
{n:'9c(iii).',q:'A 9.5 m wire falls at 4.2 m/s across B = 0.35 × 10⁻⁴ T. Find the emf',a:'emf = BLv = 0.35 × 10⁻⁴ × 9.5 × 4.2 = <b>1.4 × 10⁻³ V</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q10 – Electric fields, atoms and radioactivity (16 marks)', qs:[
{n:'10a(i).',q:'Two similarities between Newton\'s law of gravitation and Coulomb\'s law',a:'1. Both are <b>inverse-square laws</b>: F ∝ 1/r².\n2. Both forces are proportional to the <b>product</b> of the two masses or charges, and both act along the line joining them (non-contact forces).'},
{n:'10a(ii).',q:'V = 5000 V, plates 2.50 cm apart. Field strength?',a:'E = V/d = 5000/0.025 = <b>2.0 × 10⁵ V/m</b>'},
{n:'10a(iii).',q:'q = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ C moves 10 cm. Show W = 9.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ J',a:'W = qEd = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2.0 × 10⁵ × 0.10 = <b>9.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ J</b>\n⚠ This only works if the drop moves 10 cm <b>along the field</b> (towards a plate). If it really moved parallel to the plates (at right angles to the field), the field would do <b>no work</b>. The question is badly worded; write the calculation above to get the marks.'},
{n:'10b(i).',q:'Describe Rutherford\'s model of the atom',a:'Most of the atom is <b>empty space</b>. Nearly all its mass and all its positive charge are in a <b>tiny, dense nucleus</b> at the centre, and the electrons orbit around it.'},
{n:'10b(ii).',q:'What causes the biggest deflections of alpha particles?',a:'A <b>near head-on approach to the positive nucleus</b>. The strong repulsion between the positive α particle and the positive nucleus can bounce it right back.'},
{n:'10b(iii).',q:'What do the deflections confirm?',a:'That the atom has a <b>small, dense, positively charged nucleus</b>. Most α particles go straight through, which shows the atom is mostly empty space.'},
{n:'10c(i).',q:'Paths of α, β and γ in a magnetic field',a:'<b>γ</b>: goes straight (no charge).\n<b>α</b> (+2): bends one way, only a little (heavy).\n<b>β</b> (−1): bends the <b>opposite way</b>, much more strongly (very light).'},
{n:'10c(ii).',q:'100 mg, half-life 8 h. Amount left after 1 day',a:'24 h ÷ 8 h = 3 half-lives\n100 → 50 → 25 → <b>12.5 mg</b>'},
{n:'10c(iii).',q:'Time until 6.25 mg remain',a:'100 → 50 → 25 → 12.5 → 6.25 is 4 half-lives\nTime = 4 × 8 = <b>32 hours</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section B – Multiple choice (40 marks)', qs:[
{n:'1.',q:'Light hits a plane mirror at 40° to the dashed line PQ. Angle of reflection and the name of PQ?',a:'<b>A</b>: 40°, normal. (PQ is at right angles to the mirror, and the 40° is measured from it. Angle of reflection = angle of incidence.)'},
{n:'2.',q:'Image of the lamp point in a plane mirror',a:'<b>B</b>: at P and virtual. The image is as far behind the mirror as the object is in front, and plane-mirror images are always virtual.\n⚠ The MOE scheme says A (real), which is wrong.'},
{n:'3.',q:'Red vs blue light',a:'<b>B</b>: red has a longer wavelength.'},
{n:'4.',q:'How to increase the fringe spacing',a:'<b>C</b>: increase the slit-to-screen distance (Δx = λL/d).'},
{n:'5.',q:'Which statement is NOT true?',a:'<b>C</b>. The area under a displacement–time graph is not acceleration.'},
{n:'6.',q:'Thrown horizontally at 10 m/s, lands after 3.0 s (g = 9.8). Speed at impact?',a:'vᵧ = gt = 9.8 × 3.0 = 29.4 m/s, vₓ = 10 m/s\nv = √(10² + 29.4²) = √964 = <b>31 m/s → C</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme says D (39 m/s), which comes from adding the components instead of using Pythagoras. That is wrong.'},
{n:'7.',q:'Firework explosion: 100 g at 8 m/s down; two 50 g pieces at 60°. Find v₁, v₂',a:'Momentum before = 0. By symmetry v₁ = v₂ = v.\nVertical: 2 × 0.050 × v sin 60° = 0.100 × 8 → 0.0866v = 0.8 → v = <b>9.2 m/s → B</b>'},
{n:'8.',q:'Free fall near Earth: gravitational field and acceleration',a:'<b>C</b>: both stay the same (g is constant near the surface).'},
{n:'9.',q:'g on Pluto = 0.66 m/s². An object weighs 6.0 N on Earth. Weight on Pluto?',a:'m = 6.0/9.8 = 0.61 kg → W = 0.61 × 0.66 = <b>0.40 N → D</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme says B (4.0 N), which is ten times too big.'},
{n:'10.',q:'Energy stored in a coconut in a tree',a:'<b>D</b>: gravitational potential energy.'},
{n:'11.',q:'320 N over 1.5 m. Work done?',a:'W = Fd = 320 × 1.5 = <b>480 J → D</b>'},
{n:'12.',q:'Pumping air into a ball at constant T',a:'<b>B</b>: pressure increases (more molecules, more collisions).'},
{n:'13.',q:'Good conductors have many loosely bound…',a:'<b>D</b>: electrons.'},
{n:'14.',q:'0.25 A at 240 V. Resistance?',a:'R = V/I = 240/0.25 = <b>960 Ω → D</b>'},
{n:'15.',q:'Right-hand rule: the magnetic force is…',a:'<b>A</b>: perpendicular to both the current and the field.'},
{n:'16.',q:'Deflection of a moving-coil galvanometer',a:'<b>D</b>: directly proportional to the number of turns (deflection ∝ BANI/k).'},
{n:'17.',q:'Neutral electroscope touched by a negative rod',a:'<b>B</b>: negative (electrons transfer by contact).'},
{n:'18.',q:'E = 8.0 × 10⁴ N/C at 2.0 m. E at 4.0 m?',a:'Double the distance → ¼ of the field: <b>2.0 × 10⁴ N/C → A</b>'},
{n:'19.',q:'Neutrons in uranium-238 (Z = 92)',a:'238 − 92 = <b>146 → C</b>'},
{n:'20.',q:'Beta decay of Cs-137',a:'<b>A</b>: ¹³⁷₅₅Cs → ⁰₋₁e + ¹³⁷₅₆Ba (the mass number stays the same and the atomic number goes up by 1).'}
]}
]},
{y:2023, src:'P2023/PHYSICS EXAM 2023.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the MOE marking scheme. ⚠ marks the scheme\'s mistakes. g = 10 m/s² unless the question needs 9.8.',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Mirrors and lenses', qs:[
{n:'1a(i).',q:'Two things needed (besides space and a screen) to see a shadow',a:'A <b>light source</b> and an <b>opaque object</b> to block the light.'},
{n:'1a(ii).',q:'Ray diagram for the image in a plane mirror',a:'Draw two rays from the box to the mirror; reflect each with i = r. Extend the reflected rays backwards (dashed) behind the mirror – they meet at the image, the same distance behind the mirror as the box is in front.'},
{n:'1b.',q:'Concave mirror, object beyond C: rays, nature of image; then convex mirror',a:'(i) Ray parallel to the axis → reflects through F; ray through C → reflects back on itself. They meet between C and F: image <b>inverted, smaller</b>.\n(ii) <b>Real</b>.\n(iii) Convex mirror: image <b>smaller (diminished)</b>, <b>virtual and upright</b>.'},
{n:'1c.',q:'Convex lens f = 10.5 cm, object 7.8 cm. Image distance, height (object 3.2 cm), nature',a:'(i) 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/10.5 − 1/7.8 = −0.0330 → <b>v = −30.3 cm</b>\n(ii) hᵢ = hₒ × |v|/u = 3.2 × 30.3/7.8 = <b>12.4 cm</b>\n(iii) <b>Virtual</b> (v negative; object inside F – magnifying glass).'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Refraction and waves', qs:[
{n:'2a.',q:'Glass → air refraction; critical angle',a:'(i) The ray bends <b>away from the normal</b> as it leaves the glass.\n(ii) The critical angle is the angle of incidence (in the denser medium) for which the angle of refraction is <b>90°</b>. Condition: light must travel from a denser to a less dense medium.'},
{n:'2b.',q:'Sound wave; amplitude; deep-water wavefronts; λ for 8 waves in 12 s at 0.6 m/s',a:'(i) <b>sound wave</b>\n(ii) Amplitude = maximum displacement of a particle from its rest position.\n(iii) In deep water the waves travel faster, so draw the wavefronts <b>further apart</b> (longer λ), same frequency.\n(iv) f = 8/12 = 0.667 Hz → λ = v/f = 0.6/0.667 = <b>0.9 m</b>'},
{n:'2c.',q:'Failures of the corpuscular model; dual nature; diffraction at boundary B',a:'(i) It could not explain <b>diffraction and interference</b>, and it predicted light travels <b>faster</b> in water (actually slower).\n(ii) Light behaves as a <b>wave</b> (interference, diffraction) and as <b>particles (photons)</b> (photoelectric effect, solar cells).\n(iii) The waves diffract again through the gap in B, spreading out as curved wavefronts with the same wavelength.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Interference and motion graphs', qs:[
{n:'3a.',q:'Overlapping diffraction patterns; superposed pulses; coherent; path difference',a:'(i) <b>Interference</b>\n(ii) At full overlap add the heights of S and T (superposition).\n(iii) Coherent = same frequency (wavelength) and a <b>constant phase difference</b>.\n(iv) <b>constructive</b>; <b>destructive</b>'},
{n:'3b.',q:'d = 4 mm, λ = 400 nm, Δx = 1 mm. Find L; new λ when Δx = 1.25 mm',a:'(i) L = dΔx/λ = (4×10⁻³ × 1×10⁻³)/(400×10⁻⁹) = <b>10 m</b>\n(ii) λ = dΔx/L = (4×10⁻³ × 1.25×10⁻³)/10 = <b>5×10⁻⁷ m = 500 nm</b>'},
{n:'3c.',q:'v–t graph: gradient; 6–9 s; displacement 3–9 s; average vs instantaneous velocity',a:'(i) <b>Acceleration</b>\n(ii) <b>Constant velocity</b> (12 m/s)\n(iii) Area 3–6 s: ½(9 + 12) × 3 = 31.5; 6–9 s: 12 × 3 = 36 → <b>67.5 m</b>\n(iv) Average velocity = total displacement ÷ total time; instantaneous velocity = velocity at one instant (gradient of the tangent).'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Kinematics, vectors, Newton\'s 2nd law', qs:[
{n:'4a.',q:'u = 3.0 m/s, a = 0.5 m/s², 10 s; velocity at 15 s',a:'(i) s = ut + ½at² = 30 + ½(0.5)(100) = <b>55 m</b>\n(ii) v = u + at = 3 + 0.5 × 15 = <b>10.5 m/s</b>'},
{n:'4b.',q:'Scalar/vector; K = 6, M = 15 at right angles: size and direction of R',a:'(i) Scalar: time or speed. Vector: acceleration or displacement.\n(ii) R = √(6² + 15²) = √261 = <b>16.2 m/s</b>\n(iii) θ = tan⁻¹(15/6) = <b>68.2°</b> from K (= 21.8° from M).'},
{n:'4c.',q:'Newton\'s 2nd law; gradient of F vs a graph',a:'(i) The acceleration of a body is proportional to the net force and inversely proportional to its mass: <b>F = ma</b>.\n(ii) Gradient = <b>mass</b>, unit kg.\n(iii) Gradient = 30 N ÷ 6 m/s² = <b>5 kg</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – Torque, momentum, projectiles', qs:[
{n:'5a.',q:'Equilibrium; seesaw 130 N at 2.3 m, Tinga at 2.5 m',a:'(i) No net force and no net torque: the body stays at rest or keeps a constant velocity.\n(ii) 130 × 2.3 = W × 2.5 → W = 299/2.5 = <b>120 N</b>'},
{n:'5b.',q:'Conservation; 4.2 kg at 4.5 m/s E + 3.8 kg at 5.5 m/s S, stick together',a:'(i) momentum is <b>conserved</b> in a collision or <b>explosion</b>.\n(ii) p_E = 18.9, p_S = 20.9 → p = √(18.9² + 20.9²) = <b>28.2 kg m/s</b> (47.9° S of E)\n(iii) v = 28.2/(4.2 + 3.8) = <b>3.5 m/s</b>'},
{n:'5c.',q:'Ball: vᵧ = 9.50, vₓ = 12.5 m/s. Arrows; time to top; max height',a:'(i) Acceleration at S: arrow straight <b>down</b>. Vertical velocity at T: arrow pointing <b>down</b>.\n(ii) 0 = 9.5 − 10t → <b>t = 0.95 s</b>\n(iii) h = vᵧ²/2g = 90.25/20 = <b>4.5 m</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Circular motion and gases', qs:[
{n:'6a.',q:'Why accelerating; centripetal force; r = 0.65 m, T = 10 s',a:'(i) Its <b>direction keeps changing</b>, so its velocity changes.\n(ii) It pulls the car <b>towards the centre</b>, keeping it on the circle.\n(iii) v = 2πr/T = 2π(0.65)/10 = <b>0.41 m/s</b>\n(iv) a = v²/r = 0.408²/0.65 = <b>0.26 m/s²</b>'},
{n:'6b.',q:'T/F; P↑ at constant T; T↓ at constant V; conversions; Charles; PV = nRT',a:'(i) 1. T  2. T\n(ii) 1. Volume <b>decreases</b>. 2. Pressure <b>decreases</b>.\n(iii) 583.2 + 273 = <b>856.2 K</b>; 1476.9 − 273 = <b>1203.9 °C</b>\n(iv) V₂ = 1.30 × 176/299 = <b>0.765 L</b>\n(v) T = PV/nR = 1.84 × 12.0/(0.539 × 0.0821) = <b>499 K</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q7 – Work, energy, heat', qs:[
{n:'7a.',q:'W = 403 J over 2.70 m; then 150 N at 42°; effect of angle',a:'(i) F = W/s = 403/2.70 = <b>149 N</b>\n(ii) s = W/(F cos θ) = 403/(150 cos 42°) = <b>3.6 m</b>\n(iii) 1. Angle decreases → work <b>increases</b>. 2. Angle increases → work <b>decreases</b>.'},
{n:'7b.',q:'0.51 kg stone falls 3.70 m',a:'(i) Eₚ = mgh = 0.51 × 9.8 × 3.70 = <b>18.5 J</b>\n(ii) v = √(2gh) = √(2 × 9.8 × 3.70) = <b>8.52 m/s</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme uses g = 10 (18.9 J and 8.60 m/s), which does not give the 8.52 m/s the question asks you to show. Use g = 9.8 here.\n(iii) Height decreases so GPE decreases; that energy changes into kinetic energy, so speed increases.'},
{n:'7c.',q:'Elastic collision; heat vs temperature; water cooling',a:'(i) Both momentum and kinetic energy are conserved.\n(ii) Heat = energy transferred because of a temperature difference (J); temperature = measure of average kinetic energy of particles (°C/K).\n(iii) ΔT = Q/mc = 991 200/(7.6 × 4200) = 31.05 → T = 95 − 31.05 = <b>64 °C</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q8 – Electricity', qs:[
{n:'8a.',q:'Good/bad conductors; T/F',a:'(i) Good: many <b>free electrons</b>. Bad: few or no free electrons.\n(ii) 1. T  2. F (resistance increases with temperature).'},
{n:'8b.',q:'emf; power; kettle 240 V 45 Ω; TV 1.5 A 150 Ω',a:'(i) emf = energy given to each coulomb of charge; power = energy transferred per second.\n(ii) Kettle P = V²/R = 240²/45 = <b>1280 W</b>; TV P = I²R = 1.5² × 150 = <b>337.5 W</b>'},
{n:'8c.',q:'5 Ω, two 6 Ω in parallel, 7 Ω; 24 V',a:'(i) 6 ∥ 6 = 3 Ω; total = 5 + 3 + 7 = <b>15 Ω</b>\n(ii) I = 24/15 = <b>1.6 A</b>\n(iii) V across the parallel pair = 1.6 × 3 = <b>4.8 V</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q9 – Magnetism and induction', qs:[
{n:'9a.',q:'Galvanometer; commutator; F on 10 cm side',a:'(i) Detects/measures small currents.\n(ii) Reverses the current every half turn so the coil keeps turning the same way.\n(iii) F = BIL = 0.32 × 6.9 × 0.10 = <b>0.22 N</b>'},
{n:'9b.',q:'Dots/crosses; B at 8 cm from 3.9 A; force between 1.5 m wires 25 m apart',a:'(i) Dots: <b>out of the page</b>. Crosses: <b>into the page</b>.\n(ii) B = 2×10⁻⁷ I/r = 2×10⁻⁷ × 3.9/0.08 = <b>9.75×10⁻⁶ T</b>\n(iii) F = 2×10⁻⁷ × 3.9 × 3.9 × 1.5/25 = <b>1.8×10⁻⁷ N</b> (attractive – same direction)'},
{n:'9c.',q:'Rod pushed through a field: resistance force; emf; changes',a:'(i) The induced current in the rod is in the field, so it feels a force that <b>opposes the motion</b> (Lenz\'s law).\n(ii) emf = BLv = 0.30 × 0.22 × 2.5 = <b>0.165 V</b>\n(iii) 1. Decreases. 2. Increases.'}
]},
{t:'Q10 – Electric fields and radioactivity', qs:[
{n:'10a.',q:'Plates 55 mm apart, 12 V, charge 0.25 µC',a:'(i) Parallel straight lines from + plate to − plate, arrows from + to −.\n(ii) E = V/d = 12/0.055 = <b>218 V/m</b>\n(iii) F = qE = 0.25×10⁻⁶ × 218 = <b>54.5 µN</b>'},
{n:'10b.',q:'Medical use; danger',a:'(i) Radiotherapy to kill cancer cells, sterilising equipment, or tracers.\n(ii) Radiation damages cells/DNA → cancer; radioactive waste and accidents/fallout.'},
{n:'10c.',q:'²³⁷₉₃A, ²³⁸₉₂M, ²³⁵₉₁I, ²³⁴₉₀T; half-life 4 h from 160 mg',a:'(i) A: 237 − 93 = <b>144</b>; I: 235 − 91 = <b>144</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme gives I = 145, which is wrong.\n(ii) <b>M</b> (²³⁸₉₂M − ⁴₂He = ²³⁴₉₀T)\n(iii) 160 → 80 → 40 → 20 → 10 → 5 = 5 half-lives × 4 h = <b>20 h</b>\n(iv) 28 h = 7 half-lives → 160/2⁷ = <b>1.25 mg</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section B – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–5',q:'f and R; TIR; fails particle theory; fringe halved; s–t gradient',a:'1 <b>B</b> f = R/2 · 2 <b>D</b> · 3 <b>C</b> (MOE) – strictly, diffraction/interference are the real failures · 4 <b>B</b> d doubles · 5 <b>B</b> velocity'},
{n:'6–10',q:'6 km there and back; momentum; free fall; Earth/Moon weight; change of state',a:'6 <b>C</b> 0 · 7 <b>B</b> 45 000/60 = 750 kg · 8 <b>D</b> constant · 9 <b>A</b> 65 × 9.8 = 637 N, 65 × 1.63 = 106 N · 10 <b>A</b> heat'},
{n:'11–15',q:'KE of 77 kg at 9.8 m/s; gas cooled at constant V; power; heat in resistor; motor effect',a:'11 <b>C</b> ½ × 77 × 9.8² = 3698 J\n12 <b>A</b> pressure decreases. ⚠ The MOE scheme says B (increases), which is wrong.\n13 <b>A</b> · 14 <b>D</b> I²Rt = 0.25 × 100 × 20 = 500 J · 15 <b>C</b> force'},
{n:'16–20',q:'Wire in horseshoe magnet; unit of charge; distance halved; Rutherford; Ru-94 → Mo-90',a:'16 <b>D</b> (use the left-hand rule on the diagram) · 17 <b>A</b> coulombs · 18 <b>C</b> four times · 19 <b>C</b> positive nucleus · 20 <b>B</b> α-particle (94 − 90 = 4, 44 − 42 = 2)'}
]}
]},
{y:2022, src:'P2022/KSSC Physics 2022.pdf', note:'Worked solutions, checked against the MOE marking scheme. ⚠ marks the scheme\'s mistakes. g = 10 m/s².',
secs:[
{t:'Q1 – Mirrors and lenses', qs:[
{n:'1a.',q:'Shadows; plane mirror image; lateral inversion',a:'(i) Light travels in <b>straight lines</b>. An opaque object blocks the light, leaving a dark area behind it where light cannot reach – the shadow.\n(ii) Draw two rays from the object reflecting with i = r; extend them back (dashed) to meet behind the mirror – the image is the same distance behind as the object is in front.\n(iii) Left and right are swapped in the image (like writing appearing backwards).'},
{n:'1b.',q:'Concave mirror f = 4.0 cm, object 1.5 cm at 5 cm',a:'(i) <b>Real</b> (object between F and C).\n(ii) 1/v = 1/f − 1/u = 1/4 − 1/5 = 1/20 → <b>v = 20.0 cm</b>\n(iii) M = v/u = 20/5 = 4 → hᵢ = 4 × 1.5 = <b>6.0 cm</b> (inverted, magnified)'},
{n:'1c.',q:'Object beyond 2F of a converging lens; then a diverging lens',a:'(i) Ray parallel to the axis → refracts through F′; ray through the optical centre P → straight on. Image between F′ and 2F′: real, inverted, smaller.\n(ii) Diverging lens: size <b>diminished</b>; nature <b>virtual and upright</b> (on the same side as the object).\n⚠ The MOE scheme says "real/inverted"; a diverging lens always gives a virtual, upright image, so that is wrong.'}
]},
{t:'Q2 – Refraction and waves', qs:[
{n:'2a.',q:'Glass → air; critical angle (n = 1.52)',a:'(i) <b>Away from</b> the normal – light speeds up in the less dense air, so it changes direction.\n(ii) sin θc = 1.00/1.52 = 0.658 → <b>θc = 41.1°</b>'},
{n:'2b.',q:'Average displacement; sound in metal; 8 waves in 5 s; speed',a:'(i) Particles oscillate equally either side of their rest position, so on average they don\'t move away from it.\n(ii) Particles in a metal are closer and more tightly bonded, so vibrations pass on faster.\n(iii) f = 8/5 = <b>1.6 Hz</b>\n(iv) v = fλ = 1.6 × 0.25 = <b>0.40 m/s</b>'},
{n:'2c.',q:'Dispersion order; corpuscular model failure; deep → shallow',a:'(i) Blue, <b>green</b>, <b>yellow</b> (shorter wavelength bends more).\n(ii) It predicted light travels <b>faster</b> in water/glass, but it is measured slower; it also cannot explain interference (e.g. Young\'s double-slit bright and dark fringes).\n(iii) In shallow water the waves slow down: draw the wavefronts <b>closer together</b> (shorter λ), bending towards the normal if the boundary is at an angle.'}
]},
{t:'Q3 – Interference and s–t graph', qs:[
{n:'3a.',q:'Diffraction; path difference at W; W and X',a:'(i) Most noticeable when the <b>gap in the barrier</b> is about the same size as the <b>wavelength</b>.\n(ii) Path difference = 5λ − 3.5λ = <b>1.5λ</b>\n(iii) W = <b>cancellation</b> (half-wavelength difference); X = <b>reinforcement</b>.'},
{n:'3b.',q:'λ = 5.8×10⁻⁶ m, d = 0.05 mm, L = 2.5 m',a:'(i) 3rd dark fringe: d sin θ = (3 − ½)λ → sin θ = 2.5 × 5.8×10⁻⁶ / 5×10⁻⁵ = 0.29 → <b>θ = 16.9°</b>\n(ii) Δx = λL/d = 5.8×10⁻⁶ × 2.5 / 5×10⁻⁵ = <b>0.29 m</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme gives 0.58 m, which is double the correct value.'},
{n:'3c.',q:'Truck s–t graph',a:'(i) <b>Velocity</b>.\n(ii) <b>Stationary</b> (constant displacement of 15 m).\n(iii) The truck is 5 m on the <b>opposite side</b> of its starting point.\n(iv) v = Δs/Δt = (−5 − 15)/(20 − 10) = <b>−2.00 m/s</b>\n(v) It is moving in the <b>opposite (negative) direction</b> – back towards and past the start.'}
]},
{t:'Q4 – Kinematics, vectors, torque', qs:[
{n:'4a.',q:'a = 3.00 m/s², u = 5.00 m/s',a:'(i) t = (v − u)/a = (22.4 − 5.00)/3.00 = <b>5.80 s</b>\n(ii) s = ut + ½at² = 5 × 10 + ½ × 3 × 100 = <b>200 m</b>'},
{n:'4b.',q:'Vectors; B − A; shortcut x',a:'(i) Any two of: acceleration, displacement, velocity, weight.\n(ii) B − A = B + (−A): reverse the arrow of A, join its tail to the head of B; R goes from the tail of B to the head of −A.\n(iii) cos 40° = 13/x → x = 13/cos 40° = <b>17.0 m</b>'},
{n:'4c.',q:'Newton\'s 1st law; metre rule pivoted at 30 cm',a:'(i) An object stays at rest or keeps moving at constant velocity unless an unbalanced (net) force acts on it.\n(ii) Clockwise = anticlockwise: 12 × (0.9 − 0.3) = F × (0.3 − 0.1) → 7.2 = 0.2F → <b>F = 36 N</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q5 – Forces, momentum, projectiles', qs:[
{n:'5a.',q:'Zero net force; net force on cylinder',a:'(i) At rest, or moving at constant velocity.\n(ii) Horizontal: 227 − 162 = 65 N right. Vertical: both 55 N forces point down = 110 N down.\nF = √(65² + 110²) = √16 325 = <b>127.8 N</b> (59.4° below the horizontal)'},
{n:'5b.',q:'Momentum; tennis ball 0.02 kg, 9.00 → −12.0 m/s; force in 0.02 s',a:'(i) p = mv, unit kg m/s.\n(ii) Take the return direction as +: Δp = 0.02 × 12 − 0.02 × (−9) = 0.24 + 0.18 = <b>0.42 kg m/s</b>\n(iii) F = Δp/Δt = 0.42/0.02 = <b>21 N</b>'},
{n:'5c.',q:'Projectile 40.0 m/s at 50°',a:'(i) Constant horizontal velocity (no horizontal force); constant vertical acceleration g downwards (path is a parabola).\n(ii) vₓ = 40 cos 50° = <b>25.7 m/s</b>; vᵧ = 40 sin 50° = <b>30.6 m/s</b>\n(iii) 0 = 30.6 − 10t → <b>t = 3.06 s</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q6 – Circular motion and gases', qs:[
{n:'6a.',q:'Centripetal force; propeller tips',a:'(i) 1. <b>towards the centre</b> 2. <b>gravitational attraction</b>\n(ii) a = v²/r = 285²/0.95 = <b>85 500 m/s²</b>\n(iii) F = ma = 18.0 × 85 500 = <b>1.54×10⁶ N</b>'},
{n:'6b.',q:'T/F; conversions; Boyle; pressure law; moles',a:'(i) 1. <b>F</b> (P is inversely proportional to V) 2. <b>T</b>\n(ii) 532 K = <b>259 °C</b>; 161 °C = <b>434 K</b>\n(iii) P₁ = P₂V₂/V₁ = 50.0 × 2.40/200 = <b>0.60 Pa</b>\n(iv) T₂ = T₁P₂/P₁ = 300 × 500/50 = <b>3000 K</b>\n(v) n = PV/RT = (12.0×10⁵ × 0.250)/(8.31 × 293) = 3.0×10⁵/2435 = <b>123 mol</b>\n⚠ The MOE scheme gives 2435 mol, which is just RT, not n.'}
]},
{t:'Q7 – Energy', qs:[
{n:'7a.',q:'Falling coconut; stone 0.4 kg at 0.55 m; KE at 12.5 m/s',a:'(i) 1. F 2. T\n(ii) Eₚ = mgh = 0.4 × 10 × 0.55 = <b>2.2 J</b>\n(iii) Eₖ = ½mv² = ½ × 0.4 × 12.5² = <b>31.25 J</b>'},
{n:'7b.',q:'Spring graph; energy stored to 3.0 m',a:'(i) Plot (0,0), (1.5, 40), (3.0, 80), (4.5, 120) – a straight line through the origin.\n(ii) E = area under the graph = ½Fx = ½ × 80 × 3.0 = <b>120 J</b> (k = 26.7 N/m)'},
{n:'7c.',q:'Effects of heating; no temperature change during change of state; mass; latent heat',a:'(i) Temperature rises / it expands / it changes state.\n(ii) The heat energy is used to <b>break the bonds</b> between particles (increasing potential energy), not to increase their kinetic energy, so temperature stays the same.\n(iii) m = Q/(cΔT) = 2 566 200/(4200 × 94) = <b>6.50 kg</b>\n(iv) Q = mL = 6.50 × 2 260 000 = <b>1.47×10⁷ J</b> (14 690 kJ)\n⚠ The MOE scheme writes 14 690 J; the unit should be kJ.'}
]},
{t:'Q8 – Electricity', qs:[
{n:'8a.',q:'Cause of current; temperature; potentials; directions',a:'(i) A potential difference (voltage) across it pushes the charges (electrons).\n(ii) Resistance <b>increases</b>.\n(iii) 1. higher 2. lower\n(iv) Conventional current: + to −. Electron flow: − to +.'},
{n:'8b.',q:'V–I graph; resistance',a:'(i) Voltage on the y-axis, current on the x-axis – a straight line through the origin.\n(ii) R = gradient = 5.69/0.60 ≈ <b>9.5 Ω</b>'},
{n:'8c.',q:'2 Ω ∥ 3 Ω, in series with 4 Ω, 12 V',a:'(i) 1/Rₚ = 1/2 + 1/3 → Rₚ = 1.2 Ω; total = 1.2 + 4 = <b>5.2 Ω</b>\n(ii) I = 12/5.2 = <b>2.31 A</b>\n(iii) V = 2.31 × 4 = <b>9.23 V</b>'}
]},
{t:'Q9 – Magnetism and induction', qs:[
{n:'9a.',q:'Motor effect; force on AB',a:'(i) A <b>force</b>.\n(ii) <b>perpendicular</b>\n(iii) F = BIL = 0.25 × 5.5 × 0.080 = <b>0.11 N</b>'},
{n:'9b.',q:'Field around wires; B at P; force between wires',a:'(i) Concentric circles around each wire; direction by the right-hand grip rule (thumb = current).\n(ii) B = kI/r = 2×10⁻⁷ × 4.2/0.10 = <b>8.4×10⁻⁶ T</b>\n(iii) F = kI₁I₂L/d = 2×10⁻⁷ × 4.2 × 4.2 × 1.0/0.15 = <b>2.35×10⁻⁵ N</b> (repulsive – opposite currents)'},
{n:'9c.',q:'Rod 0.20 m, 30 Ω, B = 3.0×10⁻³ T, v = 3.50 m/s',a:'(i) The rod cuts magnetic field lines, so the free electrons in it feel a force – an emf is induced and current flows in the closed circuit.\n(ii) V = BLv = 3.0×10⁻³ × 0.20 × 3.50 = <b>2.1×10⁻³ V</b>\n(iii) I = V/R = 0.0021/30 = <b>7.0×10⁻⁵ A</b>\n(iv) DC gives a constant field; a transformer needs a <b>changing</b> magnetic field to induce voltage in the secondary coil.'}
]},
{t:'Q10 – Electric fields and the atom', qs:[
{n:'10a.',q:'4.5 V across 0.5 m nichrome',a:'(i) E = V/d = 4.5/0.5 = <b>9.0 V/m</b>\n(ii) The field is uniform, so the force is <b>constant</b> all along the wire: F = Eq.\n(iii) W = Eqd → d = 10.8×10⁻²⁰/(9.0 × 1.6×10⁻¹⁹) = <b>0.075 m</b>'},
{n:'10b.',q:'Rutherford; neutrons',a:'(i) Repulsion between the positive α-particles and the positive <b>nucleus</b>.\n(ii) The atom is mostly <b>empty space</b> with a tiny, dense, positive nucleus.\n(iii) Neutrons = mass number − atomic number: A = <b>24</b>, B = <b>26</b> (scheme values – read them from the nuclide symbols in the paper).'},
{n:'10c.',q:'Th-230 decay; half-life',a:'(i) ²³⁰₉₀Th → ²²⁶₈₈Ra + <b>⁴₂He</b> (Ra is radium.)\n(ii) 60 → 30 → 15 mg = 2 half-lives in 3.0 h → half-life = <b>1.5 h</b>\n(iii) 6.0 h = 4 half-lives → 60/2⁴ = <b>3.75 mg</b>'}
]},
{t:'Section B – Multiple choice', qs:[
{n:'1–10',q:'',a:'1 <b>A</b> · 2 <b>C</b> half height · 3 <b>B</b> · 4 <b>B</b> fringes closer · 5 <b>A</b> · 6 <b>D</b> v = 2.5 × 40 = 100 m/s · 7 <b>D</b> 2500 × 55 = 137 500 · 8 <b>A</b> · 9 <b>C</b> · 10 <b>D</b>'},
{n:'11–20',q:'',a:'11 <b>C</b> 350 × 1.5 = 525 J · 12 <b>B</b> · 13 <b>C</b> · 14 <b>D</b> R = P/I² = 600/4 = 150 Ω · 15 <b>B</b> commutator · 16 <b>A</b> · 17 <b>D</b> · 18 <b>A</b> E ∝ 1/r²: 9×10⁴/9 = 1.0×10⁴ · 19 <b>C</b> · 20 <b>B</b> α < β < γ'}
]}
]}
]});
